#!/bin/sh
#
# charon-sync: keep a local cache in step with its SOURCE using Unison. The
# source is canonical; the cache is the fast, offline-usable copy that working
# dirs read from. How a source is realised (an rclone FUSE mount, an fstab
# entry, a plain directory) is the PROVIDER's business, asked through
# charon-source: this half never learns the answer's shape.
#
# CONFIG IS DATA, in two kinds, both KEY=VALUE and read literally.
#
# A SOURCE (sources.d/<name>.conf) is one tree to cache, and how it exists:
#   MOUNT=~/gdrive              the source root
#   CACHE_ROOT=~/.gdrive        the local cache root
#   PROVIDER=rclone             who brings the source up: rclone, or none
#   REMOTE=gdrive               provider config (rclone remote name)
# The 'default' source is IMPLICIT, synthesized from CHARON_REMOTE, so a
# single-remote install needs NO sources.d file at all.
#
# A PROFILE (profiles.d/<name>.conf) is one subtree to reconcile, and the
# policy for it:
#   SOURCE=gdrive:Media         <source>:<subtree>  (or the legacy SUBTREE=)
#   INTERVAL=30m BOOT=12m JITTER=5m   unison timer cadence
#   SEED_PRIORITY=Media/Images/wallpaper  bulk-seeded FIRST, at install
#   SEED_FULL_ORDER=20          join the background full seed, ascending
#   The three TIER 1 policy knobs, charon's own vocabulary:
#   IGNORE=*.tmp                exclude; repeatable. A value with '/' is a path
#                               from the root, otherwise a name at any depth
#   CONFLICT=remote|local|newer which side wins when both changed (def remote)
#   DELETE=propagate|never      whether a deletion is mirrored (def propagate)
#
# WHY the split: several profiles share one source (Media and Documents both
# live under one gdrive mount), so the mount and remote name are ONE fact. Put
# them on the profile and two profiles can disagree about the same mount.
#
# There is deliberately NO working-link key. charon manages a cache; where a
# desktop surfaces that cache is LAYOUT, which belongs to the integrator.
#
# Backend TRAITS (case sensitivity, whether mtimes stick, permissions,
# symlinks) are MEASURED per source, not declared, and the prf is DERIVED from
# them: see `charon source probe`. They are not in the profile because they are
# facts about what the backend IS, not user preference.
#
# charon ships share/charon/example.conf and `setup.sh bootstrap` copies it into
# an empty profiles.d; an integrator writes its own <name>.conf files.
#
# Usage (via the `charon` front, or run this impl directly):
#   charon sync                  # same as 'run' (sync all profiles)
#   charon sync run [profile]    # sync one profile, or all (default)
#   charon sync seed             # bulk-seed the priority subtrees now
#   charon sync seed-full        # bulk-seed the full configured tree, fast
#   charon sync install          # profiles, units/timers, and the seed
#   charon sync config           # show sources, profiles, and artifacts
#   charon sync check            # systemd + notify health

# Default WARN. Assigned with := so the ENVIRONMENT still wins: it used to be a
# bare assignment, which silently discarded LOG_LEVEL=4 from a caller and made
# live debugging far harder than it needed to be.
# The template blocks at the end of this file are DATA read back out of it,
# not code. They must still parse as shell (dash -n reads past `exit 0`), but
# a static analyser reads `[Unit]` as a broken test expression. dash -n is
# the authoritative parse check here, so silencing that confusion costs us
# nothing real.
# shellcheck disable=SC1035,SC1072,SC1073,SC1020
: "${LOG_LEVEL:=2}"   # 1=ERROR; 2=WARN; 3=INFO; 4=TRACE
# logger + CHARON_REMOTE/MOUNT/CACHE/CONFIG, shared with charon-mount (a sibling
# in libexec). Reached via the `charon` dispatcher, or run directly.
_self=$(readlink -f "$0" 2>/dev/null || echo "$0")
# Where the sibling impls live (alongside this file); common_lib is in ../lib.
# CHARON_LIBEXEC overrides it so a unit test can SOURCE this file, where $0 is
# the test's shell rather than the script and self-location cannot work.
CHARON_LIBEXEC=${CHARON_LIBEXEC:-$(dirname "$_self")}
CHARON_LIB=${CHARON_LIB:-$CHARON_LIBEXEC/../lib}
. "$CHARON_LIB/common_lib" 2>/dev/null || {
  echo "charon-sync: cannot source common_lib" >&2; exit 1; }

UNISON_DIR="${UNISON_DIR:-$HOME/.unison}"
SYSTEMD_USER_DIR="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/systemd/user"
PROFILES_DIR="$CHARON_CONFIG/profiles.d"
EXAMPLE_CONF="$CHARON_LIBEXEC/../share/charon/example.conf"

LOCKDIR="$HOME/.cache"
LOCKFILE="$LOCKDIR/charon-sync.lock"

# Which paths a profile is currently failing on, per profile. STATE, not cache,
# for the same reason traits are: this is a record of what a real run observed,
# and a cleared cache must not silently erase the only evidence of a fault.
FAILED_DIR="$CHARON_STATE/failed"
# GLOBAL settings: the handful of facts that belong to the INSTALL rather than
# to any one source or profile. Sources and profiles each answer "which tree",
# and neither is the right home for "do I want the daily sweep at all".
#
# WHY A CONFIG FILE AND NOT AN INSTALL-TIME ENV SEAM. CHARON_NOTIFY is
# deliberately the latter: it is integrator policy, it is baked into the units,
# and check audits the BAKED value by reading it back out of the artifact. That
# trick CANNOT work for SWEEP, because when the sweep is off there IS NO UNIT,
# so "no timer" would be ambiguous between "off on purpose" and "never
# installed", and check could not assert either. A declaration that check can
# read in BOTH states has to live outside the artifacts it governs.
CHARON_CONFIG_FILE=${CHARON_CONFIG_FILE:-$CHARON_CONFIG/charon.conf}

# Read one KEY from the global config, literally (nothing executed), or nothing.
config_get() {   # <KEY>
  [ -f "$CHARON_CONFIG_FILE" ] || return 0
  sed -n "s/^$1=//p" "$CHARON_CONFIG_FILE" | head -1
}

# After how many CONSECUTIVE identical failures charon tries to unwedge a
# profile itself. 0 disables it. Default 3, which on a 30m profile is about 90
# minutes before it intervenes: enough passes to be sure this is not a one-off,
# far short of the four hours the 2026-09-24 incident actually ran for.
unwedge_after() {
  _ua=${CHARON_UNWEDGE_AFTER:-$(config_get UNWEDGE_AFTER)}
  _ua=${_ua:-3}
  case $_ua in
    *[!0-9]*|'')
      log_warn "UNWEDGE_AFTER='$_ua' is not a whole number; using 3"
      printf '3' ;;
    *) printf '%s' "$_ua" ;;
  esac
}

# daily (the default) or off. An unrecognised value WARNS and behaves as daily:
# leaving litter on someone's remote is the worse failure, and a typo should not
# quietly turn a safety feature off.
sweep_mode() {
  _sm=${CHARON_SWEEP:-$(config_get SWEEP)}
  case ${_sm:-daily} in
    daily)  printf 'daily' ;;
    weekly) printf 'weekly' ;;
    off)    printf 'off' ;;
    *)      log_warn "SWEEP='$_sm' is not 'daily', 'weekly' or 'off';"\
                     "treating as daily"
            printf 'daily' ;;
  esac
}

# Is a sweep SCHEDULED at all? Asked in three places, so it is one function
# rather than three comparisons against a list that has already grown once:
# adding a cadence must not mean hunting for every `= off` in the file.
sweep_scheduled() { [ "$(sweep_mode)" != off ]; }

# How old a temp must be before `sweep` will delete it. Env wins, then the
# global config, then the built-in day. See the sweep section for why an age
# gate is the safety property at all.
#
# A NON-NUMERIC VALUE REFUSES rather than coerces, and that is not pedantry:
# every "safe looking" coercion here is catastrophic, because a value that lands
# as 0 makes `find -mmin +0` match EVERYTHING, including the fresh temps that
# are live resume points. A typo must stop the sweep, never widen it.
crumb_age_min() {
  _cam=${CHARON_CRUMB_AGE_MIN:-$(config_get CRUMB_AGE_MIN)}
  _cam=${_cam:-1440}
  case $_cam in
    *[!0-9]*|'')
      log_error "CRUMB_AGE_MIN='$_cam' is not a whole number of minutes;"\
                "refusing to sweep, because a bad age gate deletes live"\
                "resume points rather than litter"
      return 1 ;;
  esac
  printf '%s' "$_cam"
}

# A SKIPPED pass is neither a success nor a fault, and conflating it with
# success cost real time: a detached archive rebuild reported `documents EXIT=0`
# and `media EXIT=0` in the SAME SECOND it started, which reads as "rebuilt" and
# actually meant "did nothing": only the absence of archive files gave it
# away. So a skip now carries its OWN exit status. 75 is EX_TEMPFAIL: try again
# later, which is exactly what a timer does. The generated unit marks 75 a
# success for systemd (SuccessExitStatus), because an offline laptop must not
# look broken, while `charon sync check` reads ExecMainStatus and reports it as
# SKIPPED rather than OK. A human or script running the verb directly sees 75
# and cannot mistake it for work done.
EX_SKIP=75

# charon SCHEDULES through systemd --user. That is an assumption, not a law,
# and when it does not hold the failure must be loud: install still writes the
# unit files (they are just text) but nothing reloads or enables them, so
# without this the install reported success while NOTHING would ever run.
# Silent success on an error path is the bug this project keeps finding.
have_systemd() { command -v systemctl >/dev/null 2>&1; }

# Optional fault-notify seam: a command supporting `flag <id> <msg>` / `clear
# <id>` / `list`. Empty (default) = no notify (soft). An integrator sets it
# (e.g. CHARON_NOTIFY=intervention-required); install bakes it into the units.
CHARON_NOTIFY="${CHARON_NOTIFY:-}"

notify() {   # <flag|clear|list> [args...]
  [ -n "$CHARON_NOTIFY" ] || return 0
  if ! command -v "$CHARON_NOTIFY" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    # CONFIGURED BUT ABSENT is drift, not "no notify". Treating it as a silent
    # no-op is how a fault reaches nobody at all: manifestor had
    # CHARON_NOTIFY=intervention-required baked into its units with the command
    # nowhere on the unit PATH, so every sync failure vanished. Say it once.
    [ -n "${_notify_warned:-}" ] || log_warn "CHARON_NOTIFY='$CHARON_NOTIFY'" \
      "is not on PATH; sync faults will NOT raise a flag"
    _notify_warned=1
    return 0
  fi
  "$CHARON_NOTIFY" "$@" 2>/dev/null || true
}

usage() {
  cat <<EOF
usage:  ${APP_NAME} [mode] [profile]

modes:
  (none)        same as 'run' (sync all configured profiles)
  run [profile] sync one profile by name, or all (default)
  seed          bulk-seed the priority subtrees from their sources
  seed-full     bulk-seed the full configured tree into the cache (fast)
  sweep [-n] [prof]
                BACKSTOP sweep of Unison transfer temps orphaned on either
                replica (each pass already cleans up after paths it saw
                settle). Only temps older than CRUMB_AGE_MIN minutes, since a
                recent one may be a live resume point. -n/--dry-run lists what
                it would remove and deletes nothing.
  audit [source]
                ask the REMOTE whether any path holds more than one object, or
                any object has no content stream. A duplicate name makes the
                mount non-deterministic and MANUFACTURES conflicts on every
                node. Records what it finds; 'check' reports that for free
                rather than paying for a listing itself.
  plan [profile]
                what the NEXT pass would do, writing NOTHING to either replica.
                Takes the sync lock, so it cannot collide with a scheduled pass.
  allow-push [--revoke] <profile>
                authorise this node to CREATE and UPDATE on the remote for that
                profile; --revoke puts it back to pull-only. Until a profile
                is authorised a pass is PULL-ONLY: it fills the cache and
                refuses to write the remote. charon does this for itself
                when it can tell the cache is safe (this node is already
                syncing, the cache is empty, or a pull-only pass withheld
                nothing); what is left is a non-empty cache of unknown
                provenance, where pushing could resurrect deleted files. Review
                'plan' first.
  install       install Unison profiles, systemd units/timers, and seed
  uninstall     remove those units/timers/profiles (NOT the cache or config)
  status        what is true now: sources, profiles, last runs, next runs
  config        alias for status
  check         check systemd + notify health

profiles come from ${PROFILES_DIR}/*.conf (see 'config').
EOF
  exit 1
}

#### profile config ####
# Names of configured profiles (basename of each *.conf), or nothing.
# EVERY *.conf basename, valid or not, one per line. Read it with
# `while IFS= read -r`, never `for x in $(...)`: the whole point is to see a
# name that would word-split, and a $()-loop destroys the evidence before the
# check can report it.
profile_names_raw() {
  [ -d "$PROFILES_DIR" ] || return 0
  for _f in "$PROFILES_DIR"/*.conf; do
    [ -f "$_f" ] || continue
    _n=$(basename "$_f"); printf '%s\n' "${_n%.conf}"
  done
}

# The names charon will ACT on: valid ones only. Filtering here rather than at
# each call site means no generator can emit a unit for a name systemd cannot
# express; check_config_sanity is what tells the user their file was skipped,
# and install refuses on it, so the skip is never silent.
profile_names() {
  profile_names_raw | while IFS= read -r _n; do
    valid_name "$_n" && printf '%s\n' "$_n"
  done
  return 0
}

# Read one KEY from a profile file, literally (nothing executed). A leading ~/
# in the value expands to $HOME.
profile_get() {   # <name> <KEY>
  _pf=$PROFILES_DIR/$1.conf
  [ -f "$_pf" ] || return 0
  _v=$(sed -n "s/^$2=//p" "$_pf" | head -1)
  # quote the pattern so the shell does not tilde-EXPAND ~/ (which would make it
  # never match the literal "~/" prefix); strip it, then prepend $HOME.
  case "$_v" in "~/"*) _v="$HOME/${_v#"~/"}" ;; esac
  printf '%s' "$_v"
}

# Read every value of KEY from a profile (one line each), for the keys that are
# legitimately repeatable. profile_get takes the FIRST only.
profile_get_all() {   # <name> <KEY>
  _pf=$PROFILES_DIR/$1.conf
  [ -f "$_pf" ] || return 0
  sed -n "s/^$2=//p" "$_pf"
}

#### sources ####
# Configured source names, plus the implicit 'default' when any profile relies
# on it (so `config` can show what is actually in play, not just what has a
# file on disk).
# Every source name that exists, valid or not. A source name arrives from TWO
# places and only one of them is a filename: the other is SOURCE=<src>:<sub>
# inside a profile, which is arbitrary text a user typed.
source_names_raw() {
  {
    if [ -d "$CHARON_SOURCES_DIR" ]; then
      for _f in "$CHARON_SOURCES_DIR"/*.conf; do
        [ -f "$_f" ] || continue
        _n=$(basename "$_f"); printf '%s\n' "${_n%.conf}"
      done
    fi
    # plus whatever profiles actually reference, which includes the implicit
    # 'default' that has no file on disk
    profile_names | while IFS= read -r _p; do
      _n=$(profile_source_name "$_p"); [ -n "$_n" ] && printf '%s\n' "$_n"
    done
  } | sort -u
  return 0
}

source_names() {
  source_names_raw | while IFS= read -r _n; do
    valid_name "$_n" && printf '%s\n' "$_n"
  done
  return 0
}

# A SOURCE is one tree to be cached, plus how it comes to exist. Profiles point
# at one; several profiles share one source, which is why this is a separate
# file rather than more keys on the profile: the mount and the remote name are
# ONE fact, and storing it per profile would let two profiles disagree about
# the same mount.
#
# Which source a profile reconciles against. SOURCE=<name>:<subtree>, or the
# legacy SUBTREE=<subtree> shorthand meaning the implicit default source.
profile_source_name() {   # <profile>
  _s=$(profile_get "$1" SOURCE)
  case "$_s" in
    "")  [ -n "$(profile_get "$1" SUBTREE)" ] && printf 'default' || : ;;
    *:*) printf '%s' "${_s%%:*}" ;;
    *)   printf '%s' "$_s" ;;
  esac
}

# The subtree under that source (may be empty: a source can be synced whole).
profile_subtree() {   # <profile>
  _s=$(profile_get "$1" SOURCE)
  case "$_s" in
    "")  profile_get "$1" SUBTREE ;;
    *:*) printf '%s' "${_s#*:}" ;;
    *)   printf '' ;;
  esac
}

no_profiles_hint() {
  log_warn "no profiles in $PROFILES_DIR"
  log_warn "bootstrap one: cp '$EXAMPLE_CONF' '$PROFILES_DIR/<name>.conf'"
  log_warn "(or run: setup.sh bootstrap)"
}

#### section extraction (embedded templates at end of file) ####
extract_section() {
  name="$1"
  awk -v name="$name" '
    $0=="__SECTION_BEGIN__ " name {flag=1; next}
    $0=="__SECTION_END__ "   name {flag=0}
    flag {print}
  ' "$_self"
}

#### what unison said, as data ####
# charon has always reported unison's EXIT CODE and never looked at a word of
# its output, so a fault said "exit 2, go read the log" and a human had to dig
# the actual paths out of the journal by hand. That is precisely what 2026-09-24
# cost: six paths, named plainly in the journal, found only by grepping it.
#
# THIS IS charon's ONLY DEPENDENCE ON UNISON'S OUTPUT FORMAT, and it is kept to
# one parser on purpose. The project's line is that unison stays replaceable, so
# a wording change must break ONE function, not a feature. A parse that finds
# nothing is NOT an error anywhere: everything built on it degrades to what
# charon did before.

# The failed paths from a captured run, one per line, deduplicated.
#
# TWO WORDINGS, because unison has two, and they are DISJOINT. All measured
# 2026-09-26 on unison 2.53.8 under the flags charon uses
# (-batch -auto -ui text -silent):
#
#  1. PROPAGATION failure, exit 2:   Failed [<relative path>]: <reason>
#     The path is BRACKETED, which is the only reason a name containing spaces
#     can be recovered, and every name that has ever caused trouble in this
#     project has had spaces. Greedy capture, so a path containing ']' survives;
#     no unison reason contains ']: '.
#
#  2. SCAN failure, exit 1:          "         error            <relative path>"
#     An unreadable file or directory, a fifo, a socket. This row is the ONLY
#     reliable source for these: the accompanying line is inconsistent, being
#     `Error in digesting <ABSOLUTE path>:` for an unreadable file, `Error in
#     scanning directory:` WITH NO PATH AT ALL for an unreadable directory, and
#     absent entirely for a fifo or a socket. Measured across all four.
#     Neither form truncates a long path; the elision with "..." that this
#     project has seen belongs to the PROGRESS display, not to these rows.
#
# Missing form 2 was a live blind spot, not a theoretical one: an exit-1 fault
# recorded no paths, so it reported nothing, built no streak and never unwedged.
#
# The one concession: form 2's row is column-padded, so a path that itself ENDS
# in a space loses it. That fails safe (a crumb lookup under a near-miss name
# simply finds nothing) and the alternative is unparseable.
parse_unison_failures() {   # <captured-output-file>
  [ -f "$1" ] || return 0
  { sed -n 's/^Failed \[\(.*\)\]: .*$/\1/p' "$1"
    sed -n 's/^ *error  *\(.*[^ ]\) *$/\1/p' "$1"
  } | sort -u
}

# Did unison reach its OWN verdict, so that its failure list is COMPLETE?
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING GUARD. Unison documents four statuses: 0 up to date, 1 some
# files skipped but transfers fine, 2 non-fatal transfer failures, 3 fatal error
# OR EXECUTION INTERRUPTED. Only 0-2 mean it finished reconciling and reported
# everything it found. A 3, or charon's own `timeout` kill (124/137), leaves a
# TRUNCATED list, and a truncated list read as "these paths are fine now" is
# the one way the machinery built on this parse could destroy something, because
# a temp still needed for a resume would look settled.
unison_rc_reported() {   # <rc>
  case "$1" in 0|1|2) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
}

# --- the record, PER PATH ----------------------------------------------------
# EACH PATH CARRIES ITS OWN first-seen and consecutive-failure count, and that
# is a correction rather than a refinement. Keying them on the whole SET looked
# tidier and was wrong in a way that defeated the feature built on top: with
#
#     pass 1  {A, B}        pass 2  {A, B, C}        pass 3  {A, B}
#
# the set differs every time, so SINCE reset every time and STREAK never left 1.
# A fault where the set SHIFTS (one path intermittently succeeding, or new
# files arriving in a broken folder) therefore reported "first seen 0s ago"
# forever and could NEVER reach UNWEDGE_AFTER. The chronic case was both
# invisible and unfixable, which is the opposite of what the counters are for.
#
# The unit of a fault is a PATH. A's history is A's, whatever B does.
#
# ON-DISK: one line per failing path, `FAILED=<streak> <since> <path>`. The path
# is LAST because it may contain spaces (and every name that has ever caused
# trouble here does); the two counters cannot, so the split is unambiguous.
failed_records() {   # <profile> -> "<streak> <since> <path>" lines
  _frf=$(failed_file "$1")
  [ -f "$_frf" ] || return 0
  sed -n 's/^FAILED=//p' "$_frf" | while IFS= read -r _fr; do
    # COMPAT: a record written by a version that stored bare paths has no
    # counters. Read it as a first sighting rather than discarding it, so an
    # upgrade mid-fault loses the history instead of losing the fault.
    case $_fr in
      [0-9]*' '[0-9]*' '*) printf '%s\n' "$_fr" ;;
      *) printf '1 0 %s\n' "$_fr" ;;
    esac
  done
}

# Just the paths. The contract every other caller reads.
failed_paths() {   # <profile>
  failed_records "$1" | while IFS= read -r _fp; do
    _fp=${_fp#* }        # drop the streak
    printf '%s\n' "${_fp#* }"   # drop the since, leaving the path
  done
}

# The paths whose OWN streak has just reached <n>. Per path, so a path that has
# failed enough is acted on whatever its neighbours are doing.
wedged_paths() {   # <profile> <threshold>
  failed_records "$1" | while IFS= read -r _wp; do
    _wk=${_wp%% *}
    [ "$_wk" = "$2" ] || continue
    _wp=${_wp#* }
    printf '%s\n' "${_wp#* }"
  done
}

failed_file() { printf '%s/%s' "$FAILED_DIR" "$1"; }

failed_get() {   # <profile> <KEY>
  _fgf=$(failed_file "$1")
  [ -f "$_fgf" ] || return 0
  sed -n "s/^$2=//p" "$_fgf" | head -1
}

# Record what a pass observed. Does NOTHING unless the list can be trusted, so
# an interrupted or timed-out pass leaves the previous record standing rather
# than replacing it with a partial one.
record_failures() {   # <profile> <unison-rc> <captured-output>
  if ! unison_rc_reported "$2"; then
    log_trace "not recording failed paths for '$1': unison exited $2 without"\
              "reporting, so its list would be incomplete"
    return 0
  fi
  _rfn=$(parse_unison_failures "$3")
  _rff=$(failed_file "$1")
  if [ -z "$_rfn" ]; then
    if [ "$2" = 0 ]; then
      # Genuinely clean: drop the record entirely, so its ABSENCE is the signal
      # that this profile has nothing outstanding.
      rm -f "$_rff" 2>/dev/null
      return 0
    fi
    # UNISON SAYS SOMETHING FAILED AND charon CANNOT NAME IT. "I parsed no
    # paths" is NOT "nothing is failing", and treating it as such used to WIPE
    # the record, including a streak that was about to unwedge a real fault.
    #
    # This is a live blind spot, not a hypothetical one: unison reports a
    # PROPAGATION failure as `Failed [path]: reason` and exits 2, but a SCAN or
    # digest failure (an unreadable source file, say) is worded differently and
    # exits 1. Measured 2026-09-26. So the record is LEFT ALONE and the gap is
    # said out loud, which is the only honest thing to do about output charon
    # does not understand, and silence here would have hidden the whole class.
    # EXCEPT while PULL-ONLY, where exit 1 with nothing attributable is the
    # EXPECTED shape: the guard makes unison skip the items it would have pushed
    # and they are not failures. run_sync_profile already warned, this pass,
    # that the profile is pull-only, and a second warning for the same expected
    # condition is the warning-that-fires-every-time problem. Still said, at
    # INFO, so a session debugging at LOG_LEVEL=3 sees the chain.
    if [ -n "${CHARON_PULL_ONLY_ROOT:-}" ] && [ "$2" = 1 ]; then
      log_info "profile '$1': exit 1 with no attributable path, which is what"\
               "the pull-only guard skipping items looks like"
      return 0
    fi
    log_warn "profile '$1': unison exited $2 but charon could not attribute it"\
             "to any path (it reports propagation failures as 'Failed"\
             "[path]:'; a scan or digest error is worded otherwise). Keeping"\
             "the previous record. See the journal for unison's own words."
    return 0
  fi
  mkdir -p "$FAILED_DIR" 2>/dev/null || return 0
  _rfnow=$(date +%s)
  # CARRY EACH PATH'S OWN HISTORY FORWARD. A path still failing keeps its
  # first-seen and advances its own streak; a path failing for the first time
  # starts at 1. A path that has STOPPED failing is simply absent from the new
  # record, which is what settled_paths reads to sweep its temps.
  #
  # The streak only advances on a pass where unison reached its own verdict (the
  # gate above), so it measures "tried properly and failed again" rather than
  # "was cut short again", which is what keeps a genuinely resuming transfer
  # from ever being unwedged out from under itself.
  _rfp=$(mktemp) || return 0
  failed_records "$1" >"$_rfp" 2>/dev/null || : >"$_rfp"
  _rfo=$(mktemp) || { rm -f "$_rfp"; return 0; }
  printf '%s\n' "$_rfn" | while IFS= read -r _rfpath; do
    [ -n "$_rfpath" ] || continue
    _rfk=1; _rfs=$_rfnow
    # Match the PATH exactly, not as a pattern: it is a filename.
    while IFS= read -r _rfrec; do
      _rfrk=${_rfrec%% *}; _rfrest=${_rfrec#* }
      _rfrs=${_rfrest%% *}; _rfrp=${_rfrest#* }
      [ "$_rfrp" = "$_rfpath" ] || continue
      case $_rfrk in ''|*[!0-9]*) : ;; *) _rfk=$((_rfrk + 1)) ;; esac
      case $_rfrs in ''|*[!0-9]*|0) : ;; *) _rfs=$_rfrs ;; esac
      break
    done <"$_rfp"
    printf 'FAILED=%s %s %s\n' "$_rfk" "$_rfs" "$_rfpath"
  done >"$_rfo"
  { printf 'LAST=%s\n' "$_rfnow"
    printf 'RC=%s\n' "$2"
    cat "$_rfo"
  } >"$_rff" 2>/dev/null || log_warn "could not record failed paths for '$1'"
  rm -f "$_rfp" "$_rfo"
  return 0
}

# One line naming the count and the oldest evidence, or nothing. Shared by the
# fault message, check and status so the three cannot describe the same state
# three different ways.
failed_summary() {   # <profile> [now-epoch]
  _fsn=${2:-$(date +%s)}
  _fsc=0; _fsk=0; _fss=
  # WORST AND OLDEST, computed per path. The counters are per-path now, so a
  # summary has to pick: the LONGEST streak, because that is the one that says
  # "this is a wedge, not a bad afternoon", and the OLDEST first-seen, because
  # that is how long the profile has actually been in trouble. Taking either
  # from a single path would let a newly-joined path reset the whole story,
  # which is exactly the flapping-set bug, one level up.
  _fsl=$(mktemp) || return 0
  failed_records "$1" >"$_fsl"
  while IFS= read -r _fsr; do
    [ -n "$_fsr" ] || continue
    _fsrk=${_fsr%% *}; _fsrest=${_fsr#* }; _fsrs=${_fsrest%% *}
    _fsc=$((_fsc + 1))
    case $_fsrk in
      ''|*[!0-9]*) : ;;
      *) [ "$_fsrk" -le "$_fsk" ] || _fsk=$_fsrk ;;
    esac
    case $_fsrs in
      ''|*[!0-9]*|0) : ;;
      *) if [ -z "$_fss" ] || [ "$_fsrs" -lt "$_fss" ]; then _fss=$_fsrs; fi ;;
    esac
  done <"$_fsl"
  rm -f "$_fsl"
  [ "$_fsc" -gt 0 ] || return 0
  printf '%s path(s) failing' "$_fsc"
  # Only from 2 up: "1 passes running" is noise.
  [ "$_fsk" -lt 2 ] || printf ', worst %s passes running' "$_fsk"
  # human_age ALREADY ends in "ago" (and can say "never" or "in the future"), so
  # this must not add another one. It read "first seen 0s ago ago" until a real
  # run put it in front of me.
  [ -n "$_fss" ] \
    && printf ', oldest first seen %s' "$(human_age "$_fss" "$_fsn")"
  return 0
}

#### state, as data ####
# These answer "what is true" and return it as DATA; the callers decide how to
# say it. check and status both need the last-run outcome, and until now check
# computed it inline, so status could not show it without a second copy of
# the logic, and the two could disagree about the same fact.

# <epoch> [now] -> "never" | "42s ago" | "7m ago" | "3h ago" | "2d ago"
# `now` is a parameter, not a call to date, so a test can pin the answer
# instead of racing the clock.
human_age() {
  [ "${1:-0}" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null || { printf 'never'; return 0; }
  _hn=${2:-$(date +%s)}
  _hd=$((_hn - $1))
  if   [ "$_hd" -lt 0 ]     ; then printf 'in the future'
  elif [ "$_hd" -lt 60 ]    ; then printf '%ds ago' "$_hd"
  elif [ "$_hd" -lt 3600 ]  ; then printf '%dm ago' $((_hd / 60))
  elif [ "$_hd" -lt 86400 ] ; then printf '%dh ago' $((_hd / 3600))
  else                            printf '%dd ago' $((_hd / 86400))
  fi
}

# <profile> -> TOKEN|EPOCH|DETAIL, where TOKEN is never|ok|skip|fail.
# The ONE place that decides what a unit's last run means. systemd calls
# EX_SKIP a success because the unit lists it under SuccessExitStatus; charon
# must not, because a pass that did nothing is not a pass that worked.
profile_outcome() {
  _ou="charon-sync@$1.service"
  _ores=$(systemctl --user show "$_ou" -p Result --value 2>/dev/null)
  _ots=$(systemctl --user show "$_ou" -p ExecMainStartTimestamp --value \
           2>/dev/null)
  _ost=$(systemctl --user show "$_ou" -p ExecMainStatus --value 2>/dev/null)
  _oep=0
  [ -n "$_ots" ] && _oep=$(date -d "$_ots" +%s 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
  if [ -z "$_ots" ]; then
    printf 'never|0|'
  elif [ "$_ores" = success ] && [ "$_ost" = "$EX_SKIP" ]; then
    printf 'skip|%s|no work done (offline, rate-limited, or lock held)' "$_oep"
  elif [ "$_ores" = success ]; then
    printf 'ok|%s|' "$_oep"
  else
    printf 'fail|%s|%s' "$_oep" "${_ores:-unknown}"
  fi
}

# <systemd time span> -> seconds. "10m" 600, "1h" 3600, "90s" 90, "2d" 172800,
# "1h 30min" 5400, a bare number is seconds. Pure, so it is unit-tested.
duration_secs() {
  # Empty in, zero out: awk never runs its main block without an input record,
  # so without this the function returned the empty string and every caller's
  # arithmetic broke with a syntax error instead of a sensible default.
  [ -n "${1:-}" ] || { printf '0'; return 0; }
  printf '%s' "$1" | awk '
    { total = 0
      s = $0
      gsub(/[0-9]+[ ]*(us|usec|ms|msec)/, "", s)   # ignore sub-second units
      while (match(s, /[0-9]+[ ]*[a-z]*/)) {
        tok = substr(s, RSTART, RLENGTH)
        s   = substr(s, RSTART + RLENGTH)
        num = tok + 0
        unit = tok; gsub(/[0-9 ]/, "", unit)
        if (unit == "" || unit ~ /^(s|sec|secs|second|seconds)$/)  m = 1
        else if (unit ~ /^(m|min|mins|minute|minutes)$/)           m = 60
        else if (unit ~ /^(h|hr|hrs|hour|hours)$/)                 m = 3600
        else if (unit ~ /^(d|day|days)$/)                          m = 86400
        else if (unit ~ /^(w|week|weeks)$/)                        m = 604800
        else                                                       m = 0
        total += num * m
      }
      printf "%d", total }'
}

# <profile> -> when the timer next fires, approximately: "~in 4m" / "due".
#
# Computed from LastTriggerUSec (a WALL-CLOCK timestamp) plus the configured
# interval, deliberately. systemd exposes the real answer only as
# NextElapseUSecMonotonic, and that is CLOCK_MONOTONIC, which EXCLUDES
# suspend, while /proc/uptime includes it. On this laptop the two differ
# by over ten hours, so comparing them called every healthy timer overdue.
# No portable shell reads CLOCK_MONOTONIC, so charon uses the clock it can
# read and SAYS the answer is approximate. RandomizedDelaySec makes it
# approximate regardless.
profile_next_run() {
  _nt=charon-sync-$1.timer
  systemctl --user is-active "$_nt" >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
    printf 'timer inactive'; return 0; }
  _nl=$(systemctl --user show "$_nt" -p LastTriggerUSec --value 2>/dev/null)
  [ -n "$_nl" ] || { printf 'not yet triggered'; return 0; }
  _nle=$(date -d "$_nl" +%s 2>/dev/null) || { printf 'unknown'; return 0; }
  _niv=$(profile_get "$1" INTERVAL); _niv=${_niv:-30m}
  _nd=$(( _nle + $(duration_secs "$_niv") - $(date +%s) ))
  [ "$_nd" -le 0 ] && { printf 'due'; return 0; }
  if   [ "$_nd" -lt 60 ]   ; then printf '~in %ds' "$_nd"
  elif [ "$_nd" -lt 3600 ] ; then printf '~in %dm' $((_nd / 60))
  else                           printf '~in %dh' $((_nd / 3600))
  fi
}

#### config sanity ####
# Two roots that are the same tree, or nested one inside the other, are not a
# misconfiguration but a catastrophe: unison would reconcile a tree against
# ITSELF, or the cache would live inside the tree it caches and sync its own
# contents into themselves, without end. charon accepted both silently and
# even generated a profile with the same root listed twice.
paths_overlap() {   # <a> <b>  -> 0 if equal, or either contains the other
  _pa=${1%/}; _pb=${2%/}
  [ -n "$_pa" ] && [ -n "$_pb" ] || return 1
  [ "$_pa" = "$_pb" ] && return 0
  case "$_pa/" in "$_pb"/*) return 0 ;; esac
  case "$_pb/" in "$_pa"/*) return 0 ;; esac
  return 1
}

# Validate the CONFIG as a whole, which no per-profile check can do: overlap is
# a property of a source, and a duplicate target is a property of a PAIR of
# profiles. Prints its own markers; 0 clean / 1 problems found.
# An unusable name is not a nuance of the config, it is a file charon cannot
# act on at all. Collected in a subshell then reported OUT here, so EVERY bad
# name is named: reporting from inside the loop would need an `exit` to carry
# the verdict out of the pipeline, and that stops at the first one.
check_names() {
  _bad=$(profile_names_raw | while IFS= read -r _bn; do
           valid_name "$_bn" || printf 'profile|%s\n' "$_bn"
         done
         source_names_raw | while IFS= read -r _bn; do
           valid_name "$_bn" || printf 'source|%s\n' "$_bn"
         done)
  [ -n "$_bad" ] || return 0
  printf '%s\n' "$_bad" | while IFS= read -r _e; do
    [ -n "$_e" ] || continue
    fail_fault "${_e%%|*} '${_e#*|}' is not a usable name. Use only"\
         "letters, digits, '_' and '-'."
    echo "         It cannot name a systemd unit, and a name with a space"\
         "splits into one phantom entry per word. It is being SKIPPED."
  done
  return 1
}

# Create the CACHE_ROOT of every source a profile uses. charon OWNS the cache,
# so creating it is its job, and doing it here removes an ambiguity that made a
# real gap uncloseable: install left it absent, so check could not treat
# a missing cache root as drift without failing every fresh install. Now
# post-install it always exists, and its later absence is unambiguously wrong.
#
# Deliberately NOT the MOUNT: for PROVIDER=none that is someone else's tree, and
# silently creating an empty directory where a missing mount belongs is how a
# dead mount gets mistaken for a live one.
# Authorise the profiles it is safe to authorise, and leave the rest PULL-ONLY
# with a loud explanation. Runs at install, which is the provisioning moment and
# the same place traits are measured.
ensure_push_authorisation() {
  for _epp in $(profile_names); do
    push_authorised "$_epp" && continue
    if push_safe_to_authorise "$_epp"; then
      authorise_push "$_epp" "install: node already syncing, or cache empty"
      log_trace "profile '$_epp' authorised to write the remote"
      continue
    fi
    log_warn "profile '$_epp' is PULL-ONLY: this node has a NON-EMPTY"\
             "cache and no unison archives, so charon cannot tell whether"\
             "that content is current or a stale restore. Pushing it could"\
             "return files the fleet has deleted."
    log_warn "  review:  ${APP_NAME%% *} plan $_epp"
    log_warn "  then:    ${APP_NAME%% *} allow-push $_epp"
  done
  return 0
}

ensure_cache_roots() {
  for _p in $(profile_names); do
    _ecs=$(profile_source_name "$_p"); [ -n "$_ecs" ] || continue
    _ecc=$(source_get "$_ecs" CACHE_ROOT); [ -n "$_ecc" ] || continue
    [ -d "$_ecc" ] && continue
    log_info "creating cache root for source '$_ecs': $_ecc"
    mkdir -p "$_ecc" || log_warn "could not create cache root $_ecc"
  done
  return 0
}

# Do the two trees a source names actually EXIST. Nothing asserted this, and a
# missing cache root changes no generated artifact, so every diff-based check
# stayed green while the cache was simply gone: fully installed, fully broken.
# (A wrong MOUNT was caught only incidentally, because it changes the rendered
# prf; deleting the cache changes nothing that gets rendered.)
#
# Existence only: a probe WRITES, and check must never write.
check_source_roots() {
  _csr=0
  for _s in $(source_names); do
    for _k in MOUNT CACHE_ROOT; do
      _v=$(source_get "$_s" "$_k"); [ -n "$_v" ] || continue
      if [ -d "$_v" ]; then
        echo "  [OK]   source '$_s' $_k present ($_v)"
      else
        # CLASSIFIED BY WHICH ROOT, because they are owned by different people.
        # install CREATES the cache root (charon owns it), so a missing one is
        # drift a re-provision fixes. It deliberately does NOT create the MOUNT:
        # for PROVIDER=none that is someone else's tree, and an empty directory
        # where a missing mount belongs is how a dead mount gets mistaken for a
        # live one. So a missing MOUNT is a fault.
        if [ "$_k" = CACHE_ROOT ]; then
          fail_drift "source '$_s' $_k does not exist: $_v"
        else
          fail_fault "source '$_s' $_k does not exist: $_v"
        fi
        _csr=1
      fi
    done
  done
  return $_csr
}

check_config_sanity() {
  _cs=0
  # NAMES FIRST: saying this before anything else stops the rest of the report
  # reading as though a skipped profile were merely healthy-and-absent.
  check_names || _cs=1
  for _s in $(source_names); do
    # A SOURCE THAT NAMES A REMOTE AND NO PROVIDER IS INCOHERENT, and it fails
    # in a way that points at the wrong thing. REMOTE is provider config for
    # rclone, and PROVIDER defaults to `none` (bring-your-own) when the line is
    # absent, so the REMOTE is silently IGNORED: charon installs no mount for
    # the source, nothing brings the tree up, and the first pass faults with
    # "source 'x' or its cache is not ready". The cause is a missing line in a
    # config file and the message is about a mount. Say the real thing.
    #
    # The implicit `default` is unaffected: it derives PROVIDER=rclone, which is
    # why a single-remote install needs no sources.d file at all.
    # THROUGH THE SEAM, like the other five call sites: source_provider() lives
    # in charon-source and is NOT in this file's scope, and `dash -n` cannot see
    # an undefined function, so the first version of this line parsed fine and
    # would have failed only at run time.
    if [ -n "$(source_get "$_s" REMOTE)" ] \
       && [ "$("$CHARON_LIBEXEC/charon-source" provider "$_s")" = none ]; then
      fail_fault "source '$_s' declares"\
           "REMOTE='$(source_get "$_s" REMOTE)'"\
           "but no PROVIDER, so it is treated as"\
           "bring-your-own and that REMOTE is IGNORED. Nothing will mount it."\
           "Add PROVIDER=rclone, or drop the REMOTE line if something else"\
           "owns that tree."
      _cs=1
    fi
    _m=$(source_get "$_s" MOUNT); _c=$(source_get "$_s" CACHE_ROOT)
    [ -n "$_m" ] && [ -n "$_c" ] || continue
    if paths_overlap "$_m" "$_c"; then
      fail_fault "source '$_s': MOUNT and CACHE_ROOT are the same tree or"\
           "nested ('$_m' vs '$_c')."
      echo "         That reconciles a tree against itself, or caches a tree"\
           "inside itself. Refusing is the only safe answer."
      _cs=1
    fi
  done
  # Two profiles reconciling the SAME pair would run two unison profiles, each
  # with its own archive, over one pair of trees, so they fight, and which wins
  # depends on timer jitter.
  _keys=
  for _p in $(profile_names); do
    _k=$(profile_source_name "$_p"):$(profile_subtree "$_p")
    [ "$_k" = ":" ] && continue
    if printf '%s\n' "$_keys" | grep -qxF "$_k"; then
      fail_fault "more than one profile reconciles '$_k'. Two profiles on"\
           "one pair of trees race, each with its own archive."
      _cs=1
    fi
    _keys=$(printf '%s\n%s' "$_keys" "$_k")
  done
  [ "$_cs" = 0 ] && echo "  [OK]   config is coherent (no overlap, no"\
                        "duplicate targets)"
  return $_cs
}

#### helpers ####
check_if_mounted() {
  if command -v mountpoint >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    mountpoint -q "$CHARON_MOUNT"; return $?
  fi
  mount | grep -q "on $CHARON_MOUNT "; return $?
}

# Ask the PROVIDER how the source is doing, tri-state, so a transient throttle
# is never mislabelled offline and turned into a fault:
#   0 online / 2 offline / 3 rate-limited.
#
# This used to run `rclone about <global remote>:` itself. That made the gate
# rclone-only and install-global: a PROVIDER=none source (fstab, automount, a
# plain directory) was probed against an rclone remote it does not have, came
# back "offline" on EVERY pass, and skipped forever, while the docs promised
# it still reconciled. The sync must not know how a source is realised.
source_health() {   # <source>
  "$CHARON_LIBEXEC/charon-source" health "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# Is the source up? Also the provider's question: charon requires a mountpoint
# where IT did the mounting, and only existence where something else owns the
# tree.
source_up() {   # <source>
  "$CHARON_LIBEXEC/charon-source" status "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# 0 if <dir> holds at least one entry (hidden ones count). A plain glob would
# report the unexpanded pattern on an empty dir, so test each candidate.
dir_has_entries() {   # <dir>
  [ -d "$1" ] || return 1
  for _e in "$1"/* "$1"/.[!.]*; do
    [ -e "$_e" ] || [ -L "$_e" ] && return 0
  done
  return 1
}

# Guard the DEAD-MOUNT signature before a two-way pass runs.
#
# A FUSE mount that has lost its backend does NOT stop being a directory: it
# answers, and presents an EMPTY one. Under a two-way sync that is
# indistinguishable from "the remote deleted everything", so the pass
# faithfully propagates the emptiness as deletions. confirmbigdel aborts when a
# whole root empties, but NOT when one subtree does while its siblings still
# read, and that partially-dead case is exactly what bit here: unison really
# did log `Deleting Images/Screenshots/screenshot_01.png from
# /home/jello/gdrive/Media`, and the file survived only in a trashed temp.
#
# The test is ASYMMETRIC on purpose. Source-empty while cache-full is the
# dead-mount signature and is refused. The REVERSE (source full, cache empty)
# is a FIRST SEED and must be allowed, or no new profile could ever populate.
check_source_sane() {   # <source> <mount-root> <cache-root> <subtree>
  if ! dir_has_entries "$2"; then
    log_error "source '$1' root '$2' is EMPTY; the source looks dead."
    log_error "refusing the pass: a two-way sync would propagate that"\
              "emptiness to the remote as deletions."
    return 1
  fi
  _sm=$2${4:+/$4}
  _sc=$3${4:+/$4}
  if [ -d "$_sm" ] && ! dir_has_entries "$_sm" && dir_has_entries "$_sc"; then
    log_error "source subtree '$_sm' is EMPTY while cache '$_sc' is not."
    log_error "that is the dead-source signature, so the pass is REFUSED"\
              "rather than propagating deletions."
    log_error "if the source really was emptied on purpose, clear the cache"\
              "subtree by hand and re-run."
    return 1
  fi
  return 0
}

# Readiness for a sync run:  0 ready / 1 fault (flag) / 2 skip this pass.
# Everything here is resolved PER SOURCE. It was global before, so a second
# source would have been gated against the wrong remote and checked against the
# wrong cache.
check_runtime_prereqs() {   # <source> <cache-root>
  source_health "$1"
  case $? in
    0) : ;;
    3) log_warn "source '$1' is rate-limited; skipping this pass -- the next"\
                "timer retries"; return 2 ;;
    # WARN, not INFO: charon-sync runs at LOG_LEVEL 2, so an INFO reason was
    # discarded and the skip became unexplainable after the fact.
    *) log_warn "source '$1' is unreachable (offline); skipping this pass"
       return 2 ;;
  esac
  if [ ! -d "$2" ]; then
    log_error "local cache dir not found: $2"; return 1
  fi
  if ! command -v unison >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    log_error "'unison' is not installed (run: ${APP_NAME} install)"; return 1
  fi
  if ! source_up "$1"; then
    log_error "source '$1' is not available (not mounted, or missing)"
    return 1
  fi
  return 0
}

check_install_prereqs() {
  log_info "checking prerequisites for install"
  # Privilege is needed for exactly ONE thing: apt-installing unison when it is
  # absent. Demanding it unconditionally made `charon install` unrunnable from
  # any non-TTY context (an agent shell, a non-interactive ssh) on a box that
  # needed no privilege at all, which turned every routine prf regeneration
  # into a sudo handoff for no reason. Gate the sudo on the actual need.
  # RUNNABLE, not packaged: see unison_present. Asking dpkg here is what made a
  # source-built or nix unison demand sudo for nothing.
  if unison_present; then
    log_trace "'unison' present; install needs no privilege"
    return 0
  fi
  sudo true || { log_error "failed to acquire sudo privileges"; exit 3; }
}

# unison_present: is the reconciler RUNNABLE. That is the only thing charon
# needs, and it is NOT the same question as "is unison a dpkg package", which is
# what both of these used to ask. A unison built from source, or installed by
# nix or brew, or living in /usr/local/bin, is invisible to `dpkg -l`, so
# charon called it absent, demanded sudo it did not need, and died in any
# non-TTY context. That is precisely the false prerequisite the sudo gate was
# added to remove; it was removed for the apt case and left in place for
# everyone else.
unison_present() { command -v unison >/dev/null 2>&1; }

install_packages() {
  log_info "ensuring 'unison' is installed"
  if unison_present; then
    log_trace "'unison' already installed; skipping"; return 0
  fi
  # Genuinely absent. apt is the only installer charon knows, so say so plainly
  # rather than running a `sudo apt-get` that does not exist on this box.
  if ! command -v apt-get >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    log_error "unison is not installed and apt-get is not available here."
    log_error "install unison by whatever means this system uses, then re-run."
    exit 1
  fi
  sudo apt-get install -y unison
}

# The prf this charon WOULD generate for <profile>, on stdout. ONE renderer,
# used by install to WRITE the file and by check to DIFF the live one against
# it, so "what is installed" and "what this version asserts" cannot drift apart
# unnoticed. That drift is not hypothetical: three interim hand-patches rode on
# both boxes' prfs and units at once, and nothing could see them.
# The backend prefs DERIVED from a source's measured traits. Emitted explicitly,
# one line per fact, rather than hidden behind unison's `fat` shorthand: `fat`
# silently bundles ignorecase, Drive is case-SENSITIVE, and that buried
# component exited every media pass on two machines for weeks. A setting that
# depends on what the backend IS should be visible and attributable.
render_prf_traits() {   # <source>
  printf '# --- derived from measured traits (charon source show %s) ---\n' "$1"
  case "$(traits_get "$1" CASE)" in
    insensitive) printf 'ignorecase = true\n' ;;
    *)           printf 'ignorecase = false\n' ;;
  esac
  case "$(traits_get "$1" TIMES)" in
    fixed) printf '# the backend cannot carry mtimes\ntimes = false\n' ;;
    *)     printf 'times = true\n' ;;
  esac
  if [ "$(traits_get "$1" PERMS)" = none ]; then
    printf '# no Unix permissions on this backend\nperms = 0\n'
    printf 'dontchmod = true\n'
  fi
  [ "$(traits_get "$1" LINKS)" = no ] && printf 'links = false\n' || :
  case "$(traits_get "$1" FSTYPE)" in
    fuse*|*fuse) printf '# %s renumbers inodes across remounts\n' \
                   "$(traits_get "$1" FSTYPE)"
                 printf 'ignoreinodenumbers = true\n' ;;
  esac
}

# CONFLICT, mapped onto unison HERE and nowhere else, so profiles never speak
# the engine's vocabulary. Prints TWO lines: the explanatory note, then the
# value for `prefer`. Split out of render_prf so it can be unit-tested without
# a full install. This mapping decides which side wins a conflict and where
# the overwritten copy lands, which is too important to reach only through an
# integration test.
prf_conflict_policy() {   # <profile> <mount-root> <cache-root>
  case "$(profile_get "$1" CONFLICT)" in
    ""|remote)
      printf '# the live mount is canonical\n%s\n' "$2" ;;
    local)
      printf '# the local cache wins conflicts (CONFLICT=local); NOTE the'
      printf ' overwritten copy then lands on the REMOTE\n%s\n' "$3" ;;
    newer)
      printf '# the newer mtime wins conflicts (CONFLICT=newer); when the'
      printf ' CACHE is newer the overwritten copy lands on the REMOTE\n'
      printf 'newer\n' ;;
    *)
      log_warn "profile '$1': unknown CONFLICT"\
               "'$(profile_get "$1" CONFLICT)'; using 'remote'"
      printf '# the live mount is canonical\n%s\n' "$2" ;;
  esac
}

# DELETE=never. MEASURED behaviour, and the comment here used to claim the
# opposite: nodeletion does NOT restore the missing copy. It stops the deletion
# propagating, then leaves the path an unresolved conflict that FAILS every
# pass until a human settles it. That is defensible, since you asked for
# deletions
# never to propagate, so charon will not guess which side you meant, but it
# is not what "never delete" sounds like.
render_prf_delete() {   # <profile> <mount-root> <cache-root>
  [ "$(profile_get "$1" DELETE)" = never ] || return 0
  printf '# DELETE=never: a deletion is not propagated either way. It becomes\n'
  printf '# an unresolved conflict until a human settles it.\n'
  printf 'nodeletion = %s\n' "$2" "$3"
}

# IGNORE, in charon's spelling: a pattern containing '/' is a path relative to
# the root, anything else matches that name at any depth.
render_prf_ignores() {   # <profile>
  profile_get_all "$1" IGNORE | while IFS= read -r _ig; do
    [ -n "$_ig" ] || continue
    case "$_ig" in
      */*) printf 'ignore = Path %s\n' "$_ig" ;;
      *)   printf 'ignore = Name %s\n' "$_ig" ;;
    esac
  done
}

# TIER 2, the escape hatch: a verbatim file included as-is, UNSUPPORTED. It
# exists so Tier 1 never has to grow a knob per engine preference. Emitted only
# when the file exists, because unison errors on a missing include, so
# creating one is a config-time act that `check` reports as prf drift until
# `install` regenerates. That visibility is the point, not a wart.
render_prf_local() {   # <profile>
  [ -f "$UNISON_DIR/charon-$1.prf.local" ] || return 0
  printf '# Tier 2 escape hatch, included verbatim and UNSUPPORTED:\n'
  printf 'include charon-%s.prf.local\n' "$1"
}

# The pair of roots a profile reconciles, as "MOUNT_ROOT|CACHE_ROOT|LABEL",
# or a failure with the reason said out loud.
resolve_profile_roots() {   # <profile>
  _src=$(profile_source_name "$1")
  if [ -z "$_src" ]; then
    log_error "profile '$1' names no source (set SOURCE=, or the legacy"\
              "SUBTREE=)"
    return 1
  fi
  # Resolve the SOURCE before the traits, so an undefined one is reported as
  # undefined. Checking traits first says "source X has no traits" about a
  # source that does not exist, sending the reader off to probe nothing.
  _mnt=$(source_get "$_src" MOUNT); _cch=$(source_get "$_src" CACHE_ROOT)
  if [ -z "$_mnt" ] || [ -z "$_cch" ]; then
    log_error "profile '$1' names source '$_src', which is not defined:"\
              "no $CHARON_SOURCES_DIR/$_src.conf, and it is not the implicit"\
              "'default'."
    return 1
  fi
  # Traits are a RENDER-time input. Refuse rather than guess: a guessed backend
  # fact is exactly what cost two machines weeks.
  if ! traits_present "$_src"; then
    log_error "source '$_src' has no measured traits; cannot render a profile."
    log_error "run: charon source probe $_src"
    return 1
  fi
  _sub=$(profile_subtree "$1")
  printf '%s|%s|charon %s:%s' "$_mnt${_sub:+/$_sub}" "$_cch${_sub:+/$_sub}" \
    "$(source_get "$_src" REMOTE)" "$_sub"
}

render_prf() {   # <profile>
  _rr=$(resolve_profile_roots "$1") || return 1
  _mroot=${_rr%%|*}; _rest=${_rr#*|}
  _croot=${_rest%%|*}; _label=${_rest#*|}
  _src=$(profile_source_name "$1")

  _cp=$(prf_conflict_policy "$1" "$_mroot" "$_croot")
  _note=$(printf '%s\n' "$_cp" | head -1)
  _prefer=$(printf '%s\n' "$_cp" | tail -1)

  extract_section "prf:charon" \
    | sed -e "s|@MOUNT_ROOT@|$_mroot|g" \
          -e "s|@CACHE_ROOT@|$_croot|g" \
          -e "s|@LABEL@|$_label|g" \
          -e "s|@CRUMB_GLOB@|$CRUMB_GLOB|g" \
          -e "s|@PROBE_DIR@|$PROBE_DIR_NAME|g" \
          -e "s|@TWIN_GLOB@|$TWIN_GLOB|g" \
          -e "s|@TWIN_GLOB_N@|$TWIN_GLOB_N|g" \
          -e "s|@PREFER_NOTE@|$_note|" \
          -e "s|@PREFER@|$_prefer|"

  render_prf_traits  "$_src"
  render_prf_delete  "$1" "$_mroot" "$_croot"
  render_prf_ignores "$1"
  render_prf_local   "$1"
}

# Probe any source a profile uses that has no traits yet. Install is the
# CONFIG-TIME moment: it is where the prf is rendered, and traits are that
# render's input. A sync pass never does this: a probe writes, and per-pass
# writes to the remote are the churn we removed.
ensure_source_traits() {
  for _p in $(profile_names); do
    _s=$(profile_source_name "$_p"); [ -n "$_s" ] || continue
    traits_present "$_s" && continue
    log_info "source '$_s' has no traits yet; probing (config time, once)"
    "$CHARON_LIBEXEC/charon-source" probe "$_s" || {
      log_error "probe of '$_s' failed; its profiles cannot be rendered"; }
  done
  return 0
}

setup_unison_profiles() {
  log_info "setting up Unison profiles in $UNISON_DIR"
  mkdir -p "$UNISON_DIR"
  for p in $(profile_names); do
    # Gate on render_prf itself, not on any one key: a profile may name its
    # tree with SOURCE= or the legacy SUBTREE=, and only the renderer knows
    # whether it resolved to a usable pair. Render to a temp first so a
    # profile that cannot resolve does not leave a truncated prf behind.
    if _out=$(render_prf "$p"); then
      printf '%s\n' "$_out" >"$UNISON_DIR/charon-$p.prf"
    else
      log_warn "profile '$p': no usable source; generated no unison profile"
    fi
  done
}

# The units this version WOULD write, on stdout. ONE renderer each, used by
# install to write them and by check to diff the live ones against them. A
# generated file that nothing diffs is invisible drift: that lesson cost a day
# on the prf, and the units had exactly the same blind spot until now. A
# hand-edited charon-mount.service went completely unnoticed by check.
render_sync_unit() {   # <resolved-notify-path>
  extract_section "unit:charon-sync@.service" \
    | sed -e "s|@CHARON_REMOTE@|$CHARON_REMOTE|g" \
          -e "s|@CHARON_NOTIFY@|$1|g"
}

# THE ORDERING DROP-IN, one per profile. The sync unit is a template instanced
# by PROFILE and the mount is a template instanced by SOURCE, so systemd cannot
# derive one instance name from the other: %i in charon-sync@media.service is
# "media", and nothing in the unit file knows that media's source is "gdrive".
# That mismatch is the whole reason multi-remote looked hard. A per-instance
# drop-in resolves it, because charon DOES know the mapping and can write it
# down at install time.
#
# Empty for a PROVIDER=none source: charon owns no unit for a tree something
# else mounts, so ordering against a unit that will never exist would leave the
# sync waiting on nothing.
render_sync_dropin() {   # <profile>
  _ds=$(profile_source_name "$1")
  [ -n "$_ds" ] || return 0
  [ "$("$CHARON_LIBEXEC/charon-source" provider "$_ds")" = rclone ] || return 0
  printf '[Unit]\n'
  printf '# generated by charon: profile %s draws on source %s\n' "$1" "$_ds"
  printf 'After=charon-mount@%s.service\n' "$_ds"
  printf 'Wants=charon-mount@%s.service\n' "$_ds"
}

# Where that drop-in lives. systemd reads drop-ins for a SPECIFIC instance from
# <instance>.service.d/, which is what makes per-profile ordering possible at
# all while the unit itself stays a single shared template.
sync_dropin_file() {   # <profile>
  printf '%s/charon-sync@%s.service.d/10-source.conf' "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR" "$1"
}

# The BACKSTOP pair: a daily blind sweep, for litter the per-pass surgical
# cleanup could not know about. It is not redundant with it, because a crash
# before
# the state file was written, litter left by an older charon, a name whose
# directory has since been renamed, or simply another machine's residue all
# reach the remote without ever appearing in this box's settled list.
render_sweep_unit() {
  extract_section "unit:charon-sweep.service" \
    | sed -e "s|@CHARON_REMOTE@|$CHARON_REMOTE|g"
}
# The cadence is substituted rather than hardcoded, so switching SWEEP
# re-renders the timer and check's diff notices. systemd accepts 'daily' and
# 'weekly' as OnCalendar shorthands directly, so the mode name IS the value.
render_sweep_timer() {
  _rsm=$(sweep_mode)
  [ "$_rsm" != off ] || return 1
  extract_section "timer:charon-sweep.timer" | sed -e "s|@CADENCE@|$_rsm|g"
}

render_timer() {   # <profile>
  _iv=$(profile_get "$1" INTERVAL); _bt=$(profile_get "$1" BOOT)
  _jt=$(profile_get "$1" JITTER)
  extract_section "timer:charon-sync.timer" \
    | sed -e "s|@NAME@|$1|g" \
          -e "s|@INTERVAL@|${_iv:-30m}|g" \
          -e "s|@BOOT@|${_bt:-10m}|g" \
          -e "s|@JITTER@|${_jt:-2m}|g"
}

# Diff one generated artifact against what this version would write.
# 0 matches / 1 drifted-or-missing. Prints its own marker line.
check_generated() {   # <label> <live-file> <rendered-tmp>
  if [ ! -f "$2" ]; then
    fail_drift "$1 missing (run '${APP_NAME} install')"; return 1
  fi
  if diff -q "$3" "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "  [OK]   $1 matches this version's template"; return 0
  fi
  fail_drift "$1 DIFFERS from this version's template (hand-edited, or a"\
       "stale install); run '${APP_NAME} install'"
  return 1
}

# RESOLVE the notify command to an ABSOLUTE path before it is baked. A --user
# unit runs with a bare system PATH: no ~/bin, no ~/.local/bin. So a BARE
# COMMAND NAME that resolves fine in the installing shell is unresolvable at
# run time, and every fault then reaches nobody. Found live on both boxes,
# which baked CHARON_NOTIFY=intervention-required while that command lives in
# ~/bin. Resolving HERE is the fix, because this shell has the full PATH.
# charon still does not care WHAT the command is: the seam stays a plain
# command contract, this only pins WHERE it is.
resolved_notify() {
  case "$CHARON_NOTIFY" in
    ""|/*) printf '%s' "$CHARON_NOTIFY"; return 0 ;;
  esac
  _np=$(command -v "$CHARON_NOTIFY" 2>/dev/null || true)
  if [ -n "$_np" ]; then
    # >&2 IS LOAD-BEARING. This function's STDOUT is its return value, read by
    # `$(resolved_notify)`, so anything it prints there becomes part of the
    # value. log_info writes to stdout, so at LOG_LEVEL>=3 the baked notify path
    # became "[INFO ] ...\n/home/jello/bin/...", which is MULTI-LINE, and a
    # multi-line sed replacement is a sed ERROR, so render_sync_unit produced
    # nothing and install, which redirects straight to the unit file, left
    # charon-sync@.service EMPTY. Measured on a live box 2026-09-26 by running
    # install at LOG_LEVEL=3. GENERAL RULE: a function whose stdout is a value
    # must never log to stdout.
    log_info "notify seam resolved to $_np" >&2; printf '%s' "$_np"
  else
    log_warn "CHARON_NOTIFY='$CHARON_NOTIFY' is not on PATH at install time;"\
             "baking as-is, but a --user unit will NOT resolve it"
    printf '%s' "$CHARON_NOTIFY"
  fi
}

# The per-profile artifacts: its timer, and the drop-in binding it to its own
# source's mount. Rewritten every install, so re-pointing a profile at another
# source moves the ordering with it; the stale directory is cleared FIRST, or a
# profile that moved to PROVIDER=none would keep waiting on a mount it no
# longer uses.
write_profile_artifacts() {   # <profile>
  install_artifact "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sync-$1.timer" \
    render_timer "$1" || return 1
  _dp=$(sync_dropin_file "$1")
  rm -rf "${_dp%/*}"
  _dd=$(render_sync_dropin "$1")
  [ -n "$_dd" ] || return 0
  mkdir -p "${_dp%/*}"
  printf '%s\n' "$_dd" >"$_dp"
}

# Write (or REMOVE) the daily sweep pair, according to SWEEP.
#
# A MODE SWITCH REMOVES THE OTHER STATE. Turning the sweep off has to delete the
# timer and the unit, not merely stop enabling them: a leftover enabled timer
# would keep deleting from the user's remote after they asked it not to, which
# is the worst way to honour a setting. Both directions are idempotent, so
# running install twice in either mode changes nothing the second time.
# Write a generated artifact by RENDERING TO A TEMP FIRST, replacing the live
# file only if the render succeeded and produced something.
#
# `renderer > "$live"` truncates the live file BEFORE the renderer runs, so a
# renderer that fails leaves an EMPTY artifact where a working one used to be.
# That is not hypothetical: a stdout-polluted notify value made
# render_sync_unit's sed fail, and install blanked charon-sync@.service on a
# live box (2026-09-26). setup_unison_profiles already rendered via a temp for
# exactly this reason: "so a profile that cannot resolve does not leave a
# truncated prf behind", and the UNIT writers never got the same treatment.
# Stderr is deliberately NOT swallowed: the renderer's own reason is the only
# clue to why it failed.
install_artifact() {   # <live-path> <renderer> [args...]
  _iap=$1; shift
  _iat=$_iap.charon-new.$$
  if ! "$@" >"$_iat"; then
    rm -f "$_iat"
    log_error "could not render $(basename "$_iap"); KEEPING the existing one"\
              "rather than replacing it with a broken file"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ ! -s "$_iat" ]; then
    rm -f "$_iat"
    log_error "rendered $(basename "$_iap") came out EMPTY; KEEPING the"\
              "existing one"
    return 1
  fi
  mv -f "$_iat" "$_iap" || {
    rm -f "$_iat"; log_error "could not install $_iap"; return 1; }
  return 0
}

setup_sweep_units() {
  if ! sweep_scheduled; then
    if [ -f "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sweep.timer" ] \
       || [ -f "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sweep.service" ]; then
      log_info "SWEEP=off: removing the sweep timer"
    fi
    if have_systemd; then
      systemctl --user disable --now charon-sweep.timer >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
    fi
    rm -f "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sweep.timer" \
          "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sweep.service"
    log_info "SWEEP=off: nothing will collect orphaned Unison temps"\
             "automatically; run '${APP_NAME%% *} sweep' when you want to"
    return 0
  fi
  _sur=0
  install_artifact "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sweep.service" \
    render_sweep_unit || _sur=1
  install_artifact "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sweep.timer" \
    render_sweep_timer || _sur=1
  return $_sur
}

setup_systemd_units() {
  log_info "setting up systemd user units in $SYSTEMD_USER_DIR"
  mkdir -p "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR"
  # The service template carries the resolved remote + notify seam, so the
  # timed --user runs (no shell env) use the config this install resolved.
  _ssu=0
  install_artifact "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sync@.service" \
    render_sync_unit "$(resolved_notify)" || _ssu=1
  setup_sweep_units || _ssu=1
  for p in $(profile_names); do
    write_profile_artifacts "$p" || _ssu=1
  done

  if ! have_systemd; then
    log_warn "systemctl not found: the unit files are WRITTEN but NOTHING IS"\
             "SCHEDULED. charon will not sync on its own."
    log_warn "run '${APP_NAME%% *} sync' from your own scheduler (cron, a"\
             "supervisor, by hand), or install on a systemd --user session."
    return $_ssu
  fi
  log_trace "reloading systemd user daemon"
  systemctl --user daemon-reload
  log_trace "enabling timers"
  # SAY SO WHEN enable FAILS. These used to be bare `>/dev/null 2>&1` with the
  # status discarded, so install reported success having scheduled nothing and
  # only a later `check` could notice, the same silent-success-on-an-error
  # path this project already fixed once for the absent-scheduler case.
  for p in $(profile_names); do
    systemctl --user enable --now "charon-sync-$p.timer" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || log_warn "could not enable charon-sync-$p.timer; '$p' will NOT sync"\
                  "on a schedule (check will say so)"
  done
  if ! sweep_scheduled; then
    return $_ssu
  fi
  systemctl --user enable --now charon-sweep.timer >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || log_warn "could not enable charon-sweep.timer; orphaned Unison temps"\
                "will not be collected automatically (check will say so)"
  return $_ssu
}

# Fast, resumable initial population of specific subtrees straight from the
# remote API with rclone (parallel, idempotent, RESUMES after interruption,
# unlike a killed unison-over-FUSE crawl, which loses all progress). Non-fatal.
# Seed one profile's subtree, THROUGH THE PROVIDER. It used to run rclone
# itself against the global remote and global cache, which made seeding
# rclone-only and blind to which source a profile actually uses. A provider
# that cannot bulk-copy (PROVIDER=none) is not a failure: seeding is an
# optimisation, and the reconciler populates the cache on its first pass.
seed_profile() {   # <profile> <subtree>
  _ss=$(profile_source_name "$1"); [ -n "$_ss" ] || return 0
  _sc=$(source_get "$_ss" CACHE_ROOT); [ -n "$_sc" ] || return 0
  [ -n "$2" ] || return 0
  "$CHARON_LIBEXEC/charon-source" seed "$_ss" "$2" "$_sc/$2" || {
    log_warn "seed of '$2' incomplete (offline or rate-limited; the timers"\
             "will finish it later)"
    return 1
  }
  return 0
}

# The priority subtrees (SEED_PRIORITY across profiles): seeded synchronously.
# Seed every profile's SEED_PRIORITY subtree, synchronously: a fresh box needs
# these now (a desktop wallpaper, say).
seed_priority() {
  _r=0
  for p in $(profile_names); do
    _sp=$(profile_get "$p" SEED_PRIORITY); [ -n "$_sp" ] || continue
    seed_profile "$p" "$_sp" || _r=1
  done
  return $_r
}

# Profiles that join the background full seed, in ascending SEED_FULL_ORDER.
full_profiles() {
  for p in $(profile_names); do
    _o=$(profile_get "$p" SEED_FULL_ORDER); [ -n "$_o" ] || continue
    printf '%s\t%s\n' "$_o" "$p"
  done | sort -n | cut -f2
}

seed_full() {
  mkdir -p "$LOCKDIR"
  exec 9>"$LOCKFILE"
  if ! flock -n 9 2>/dev/null; then
    # WARN, not INFO: charon-sync runs at LOG_LEVEL 2, so an INFO reason is
    # DISCARDED and a seed that did nothing says nothing at all. `charon
    # install` kicks this, so the user would be told their cache was seeded
    # when it was not. Exactly the discarded-reason bug already fixed for the
    # offline skip; this call site was missed.
    log_warn "seed-full: a sync holds the lock; skipping (timers will populate)"
    return 0
  fi
  _rc=0
  for p in $(full_profiles); do
    _fs=$(profile_subtree "$p"); [ -n "$_fs" ] || continue
    log_info "seed-full: bulk-seeding '$_fs' for profile '$p'"
    seed_profile "$p" "$_fs" || _rc=1
  done
  flock -u 9 2>/dev/null; exec 9>&-       # release BEFORE kicking Unison
  # Kick the profiles ONE AT A TIME, and WAIT for each. All profiles share a
  # single sync lock (overlapping unison runs would contend on the mount and on
  # each other's archives), so starting them all with --no-block guaranteed
  # that every profile after the first hit the lock and recorded a SKIPPED run.
  # That made an install reliably leave a spurious warning behind, and a
  # warning you see after every install is a warning you stop reading.
  for p in $(profile_names); do
    systemctl --user start "charon-sync@$p.service" || true
  done
  log_info "seed-full: bulk seed finished (rc $_rc); unison reconcile kicked"
  return "$_rc"
}

#### sweeping unison's orphaned transfer temps ####
# WHY THIS IS A SWEEP AND NOT A PREVENTION (all of it MEASURED 2026-09-25 in a
# scratch pair; test/sweep.t reproduces the load-bearing parts).
#
# A crumb is NOT unison state. Unison's state (the archives) already lives
# wholly outside both replicas in UNISON_DIR, so none of it reaches the remote.
# `.unison.<name>.<hash>.unison.tmp` is the IN-FLIGHT CONTENT of a file being
# written to the destination, staged in the destination DIRECTORY so the last
# step can be an atomic rename(). rename() does not cross filesystems, so the
# temp for a remote-bound transfer cannot be staged locally without giving up
# atomicity: a partially written file would be visible at the real name, on
# the remote. Unison offers no preference to relocate it, and the temp doubles
# as the RESUME point (the manual: copyprogrest is used "when Unison sees a
# partially transferred temp file on the receiving host").
#
# Unison DELETES its own temp the next time it propagates that path
# SUCCESSFULLY, with the Tier 0 ignore set or not, so the ignore never
# blocked cleanup. A temp therefore becomes permanent litter in exactly one
# situation: the path stops needing propagation while a temp is outstanding.
# That is what a WEDGED profile manufactures. Every pass fails so the temp is
# never consumed, and once the replicas agree unison reports "Nothing to do"
# and never revisits the path again. That is the whole of our 2026-09-24
# incident, so the real fix upstream of this verb is to stop profiles wedging.
#
# Hence: out-of-band by necessity, AGE-GATED by safety. A recent temp may be
# the live resume point of an interrupted transfer. Deleting one is never
# destructive (it costs a re-transfer, and it is by definition not content),
# but there is no reason to pay that cost, and on a SHARED remote the
# outstanding transfer may be another box's. The gate is crumb_age_min(), whose
# value the user owns (CRUMB_AGE_MIN in charon.conf, or the env).

# --- the surgical half: clean up the instant a path is known to have ----------
# The blind sweep below can only ask "is this old enough to be safe?", because
# it has no idea whether unison still wants a given temp. After a pass, charon
# DOES know, and this is where that knowledge gets spent.
#
# WHY "SETTLED" AND NOT "FAILED". The obvious trigger is the wrong one: clean up
# the paths that just FAILED. At that moment the temp is most likely to be a
# legitimate RESUME POINT: it exists precisely so the next pass continues
# instead of re-transferring, which on a cold FUSE tree is the difference
# between seconds and an hour. And the orphaning condition is "the path stopped
# needing propagation while a temp was outstanding", which is NOT OBSERVABLE at
# failure time: a path that just failed still needs propagating.
#
# The observable moment is the one AFTER: a path that was failing and is not in
# the new failure list has settled. Either unison propagated it (and consumed
# its own temp, so there is nothing to find) or it no longer needs propagating
# at all, which is the orphan case exactly, and now provable rather than
# guessed. So no age gate is needed here: this is not "probably litter by
# tomorrow", it is litter now.

# The paths that WERE failing and are not any more, one per line. Pure: no
# logging, no deleting, so it can be tested on its own.
settled_paths() {   # <profile> <new-failure-set-file, sorted>
  [ -f "$(failed_file "$1")" ] || return 0
  failed_paths "$1" | sort -u | comm -23 - "$2"
}

# Remove the crumbs belonging to ONE path, on BOTH replicas.
#
# MATCHED LITERALLY, NOT BY GLOB. The obvious spelling is
# `"$dir/.unison.$base."*.unison.tmp`, but $base is a FILENAME and the shell
# would read any '[', '*' or '?' in it as pattern syntax, so the one class of
# name this project keeps getting bitten by would silently not match. Listing
# the directory and comparing strings cannot misread a name. It fails SAFE
# either way (a miss leaves litter for the blind sweep; it can never delete the
# wrong file), but a silent miss is exactly the kind of thing nobody notices.
# The crumbs belonging to ONE path under ONE root, one per line. PURE: no
# logging, no deleting, so the same matcher serves both the code that removes
# them and the code that needs to know whether there is anything to remove.
path_crumbs() {   # <root> <relative-path>
  _pcb=$(basename -- "$2")
  _pcdir=$1/$(dirname -- "$2")
  [ -d "$_pcdir" ] || return 0
  for _pcc in "$_pcdir"/.unison.*.unison.tmp; do
    [ -f "$_pcc" ] || continue
    # .unison.<name>.<hash>.unison.tmp -> <name>, with the hash being the LAST
    # dot-component (a name of its own may well contain dots).
    _pcm=$(basename -- "$_pcc"); _pcm=${_pcm#.unison.}
    _pcm=${_pcm%.unison.tmp}; _pcm=${_pcm%.*}
    [ "$_pcm" = "$_pcb" ] || continue
    printf '%s\n' "$_pcc"
  done
}

sweep_path_crumbs() {   # <mount-root> <cache-root> <relative-path> [reason]
  for _spr in "$1" "$2"; do
    [ -n "$_spr" ] || continue
    # The REASON is a parameter because two callers share this: a path that
    # SETTLED (its temp is now litter) and a path being UNWEDGED (its temp has
    # had its chances and is in the way). Logging both as "settled" would
    # describe the second one as the opposite of what it is.
    path_crumbs "$_spr" "$3" | while IFS= read -r _spc; do
      if rm -f -- "$_spc" 2>/dev/null; then
        log_info "${4:-settled} '$3': removed transfer temp $_spc"
      else
        log_warn "${4:-settled} '$3': could NOT remove $_spc"
      fi
    done
  done
  return 0
}

# --- unwedging a profile that keeps failing the SAME way ---------------------
# THE PROBLEM THIS EXISTS FOR (2026-09-24, four hours, both boxes). A profile
# can fail the identical propagation every pass, forever: the attempt cannot
# succeed, so unison never commits the archive, so the next pass tries exactly
# the same thing. charon faulted loudly and correctly each time and the fault
# never cleared, because nothing charon did could clear it, and neither could
# an integrator, since `install` cannot change what a past run returned. A human
# had to run a pass by hand. Everything built since narrowed the blast radius;
# none of it stopped a profile wedging.
#
# THE ONE CAUSE WE CAN ACT ON is a stranded transfer temp. Measured in a scratch
# pair 2026-09-25: a stranded temp is ITSELF enough to make the next pass exit 2
# with "Destination updated during synchronization", reproducibly on one box and
# never on another. So the temp, whose whole purpose is to help the next
# attempt, can be what defeats it.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT THE AGE GATE AGAIN, and why it is safe to be more aggressive:
# the sweep's gate exists because a RECENT temp is probably a live resume point.
# For a path that has now failed N CONSECUTIVE COMPLETED passes, that premise is
# measurably false: the resume point has had N chances and helped none of
# them. Critically, an interrupted pass does NOT advance the streak (its rc is
# not trusted), so a path that is genuinely resuming across several long
# transfers never reaches the threshold this way. The streak only counts "unison
# finished, looked, and reported this path failed again".
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO: touch the archive. Invalidating a unison
# archive forces a cold rescan of every file through FUSE, which the notes
# measured at roughly an hour per profile and which loses all progress if
# killed. That is a cure worse than the disease. Dropping a temp costs at most
# one re-transfer of one file, and a temp is never content.
#
# ONCE PER FAULT, at the moment the streak CROSSES the threshold. Acting on
# every later pass would be a no-op (the temps are gone) that logged a warning
# every time, and a warning that fires every time is one nobody reads.
unwedge_profile() {   # <profile>
  _uwa=$(unwedge_after)
  # The 0 test is DEFENCE IN DEPTH, not load-bearing, and mutation testing says
  # so honestly: a streak starts at 1 and wedged_paths only ever compares EQUAL,
  # so 0 could never match anyway. Kept because it states the intent of 0 and
  # would still hold if a streak ever started counting from zero.
  [ "$_uwa" -gt 0 ] || return 0
  # PER PATH. It used to take the whole profile's streak and act on every
  # failing path at once, which meant a single newly-joined path reset the count
  # for all of them and nothing was ever unwedged. Now each path reaches the
  # threshold on its own history, and only those are acted on.
  set -- "$1" "$(wedged_paths "$1" "$_uwa")"
  [ -n "$2" ] || return 0
  # NAME THE PATHS IN THE WARNING ITSELF. The per-path removals log at INFO, and
  # charon-sync pins LOG_LEVEL=2, so in production those lines are SUPPRESSED,
  # a warning saying only "2 paths" would be charon deleting from someone's
  # remote on its own initiative without saying which files. Bounded to three so
  # a wide fault cannot flood the journal.
  _uwn=$(printf '%s\n' "$2" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
  _uwl=$(printf '%s\n' "$2" | head -3 | tr '\n' ';' | sed 's/;$//')
  [ "$_uwn" -le 3 ] || _uwl="$_uwl; ... and $((_uwn - 3)) more"

  # COUNT WHAT THERE IS TO DROP BEFORE CLAIMING TO DROP IT. Dropping a temp is
  # the only lever charon has, and it does not fit every wedge: a SCAN failure
  # (an unreadable file, a fifo, a socket) has no temp at all, and nor does a
  # propagation failure that never got as far as writing one. Announcing an
  # intervention and then silently doing nothing is the worst of both, so when
  # there is nothing to drop charon says THAT instead, which is itself the
  # useful signal: this profile is wedged and charon cannot help.
  _uwr=$(resolve_profile_roots "$1" 2>/dev/null) || return 0
  _uwm=${_uwr%%|*}; _uwt=${_uwr#*|}; _uwc=${_uwt%%|*}
  _uwf=$(mktemp) || return 0
  printf '%s\n' "$2" | while IFS= read -r _uwp; do
    [ -n "$_uwp" ] || continue
    path_crumbs "$_uwm" "$_uwp"
    path_crumbs "$_uwc" "$_uwp"
  done >"$_uwf"
  _uwk=$(wc -l <"$_uwf" | tr -d ' ')
  rm -f "$_uwf"

  if [ "$_uwk" -eq 0 ]; then
    log_warn "profile '$1' has $_uwn path(s) failing $_uwa passes running and"\
             "charon has NOTHING it can do: there are no transfer temps to"\
             "drop, so this is not a stale-temp wedge and it needs a human."\
             "Paths: $_uwl"
    return 0
  fi
  log_warn "profile '$1' has $_uwn path(s) failing $_uwa passes running;"\
           "dropping $_uwk transfer temp(s) so the next pass starts clean (a"\
           "temp is never content, and these have had $_uwa chances to help):"\
           "$_uwl"
  sweep_settled "$1" "$2" unwedge
  return 0
}

# Clean up after every path a pass showed to have settled.
#
# ASSUMES THE CALLER HOLDS THE SYNC LOCK, and must: it runs from inside a pass,
# which took the lock in run_with_lock and holds it for the whole pass. Taking
# it again here would skip every time.
sweep_settled() {   # <profile> <paths, newline separated> [reason]
  [ -n "$2" ] || return 0
  _ssr=$(resolve_profile_roots "$1" 2>/dev/null) || return 0
  _ssm=${_ssr%%|*}; _sst=${_ssr#*|}; _ssc=${_sst%%|*}
  _ssw=${3:-settled}
  printf '%s\n' "$2" | while IFS= read -r _ssp; do
    [ -n "$_ssp" ] || continue
    sweep_path_crumbs "$_ssm" "$_ssc" "$_ssp" "$_ssw"
  done
  return 0
}

# Orphaned crumbs under one root, one per line. Callers MUST read this with
# `while IFS= read -r` from a redirect, never `for x in $(...)` and never
# through a pipe: these are FILENAMES (this Drive is full of spaces) and a
# pipe would put the reader's counters in a subshell where they cannot survive.
# The age is a PARAMETER, not read from ambient state here: a function that
# reaches for the environment cannot be tested across its own boundary, and this
# one's boundary is the difference between deleting litter and deleting a live
# resume point.
crumbs_in() {   # <root> <age-minutes>
  [ -d "$1" ] || return 0
  find "$1" -type f -name "$CRUMB_GLOB" -mmin "+$2" 2>/dev/null
}

# Both roots of every profile named, or of all of them. Scoped to the
# configured subtrees on purpose: charon reports on the trees it manages, not
# on whatever else shares the remote.
crumb_roots() {   # [profile]
  for _crp in ${1:-$(profile_names)}; do
    _crr=$(resolve_profile_roots "$_crp" 2>/dev/null) || continue
    printf '%s\n' "${_crr%%|*}"                    # mount side
    _crt=${_crr#*|}; printf '%s\n' "${_crt%%|*}"   # cache side
  done
}

# count and total bytes of the orphaned crumbs, as "<count> <bytes>".
# Count paths arriving on stdin: "<count> <bytes>" on one line. Extracted so
# the crumb tally and the twin tally cannot drift apart on what counting means.
tally_paths() {
  _tpn=0; _tpb=0
  while IFS= read -r _tpf; do
    [ -n "$_tpf" ] || continue
    _tps=$(stat -c %s "$_tpf" 2>/dev/null) || _tps=0
    _tpn=$((_tpn + 1)); _tpb=$((_tpb + _tps))
  done
  printf '%s %s' "$_tpn" "$_tpb"
}

crumb_tally() {   # [profile]
  _cta=$(crumb_age_min) || return 1
  crumb_roots "${1:-}" | while IFS= read -r _ctr; do
    crumbs_in "$_ctr" "$_cta"
  done | tally_paths
}

# A conflict copy, on ONE side. No age gate: a twin is a completed file, never
# a live resume point, so age says nothing about it (unlike a transfer temp).
twins_in() {   # <root>
  [ -d "$1" ] || return 0
  find "$1" -type f \( -name "$TWIN_GLOB" -o -name "$TWIN_GLOB_N" \) \
    2>/dev/null
}

# Twins per SIDE, because the two sides mean opposite things and one report
# cannot serve both. <mount-count> <mount-bytes> <cache-count> <cache-bytes>.
twin_tally() {   # [profile]
  _ttm=$(for _ttp in ${1:-$(profile_names)}; do
           _ttr=$(resolve_profile_roots "$_ttp" 2>/dev/null) || continue
           twins_in "${_ttr%%|*}"
         done | tally_paths)
  _ttc=$(for _ttp in ${1:-$(profile_names)}; do
           _ttr=$(resolve_profile_roots "$_ttp" 2>/dev/null) || continue
           _ttt=${_ttr#*|}; twins_in "${_ttt%%|*}"
         done | tally_paths)
  printf '%s %s' "$_ttm" "$_ttc"
}

# Delete them. Under the sync LOCK, because a pass in flight owns its temps and
# a crumb that is a live resume point must not be pulled out from under it.
do_sweep() {   # [-n|--dry-run] [profile]
  # A DRY RUN IS THE FIRST THING A CAUTIOUS USER REACHES FOR, and rightly: this
  # deletes files from someone's remote on a timer. It is also what you want on
  # a new box before trusting the age gate on a tree you have not looked at.
  _swd=
  case ${1:-} in
    -n|--dry-run) _swd=1; shift ;;
  esac
  _swp=${1:-}
  if [ -n "$_swp" ] && [ ! -f "$PROFILES_DIR/$_swp.conf" ]; then
    log_error "unknown profile '$_swp'"; return 1
  fi
  # Resolve the age BEFORE taking the lock, so a bad value refuses without
  # holding up a pass that is waiting behind it.
  _swa=$(crumb_age_min) || return 2
  mkdir -p "$LOCKDIR"
  exec 9>"$LOCKFILE"
  if ! flock -n 9 2>/dev/null; then
    # WARN, not INFO: charon-sync pins LOG_LEVEL 2, so an INFO reason is
    # discarded and a sweep that did nothing would say nothing at all.
    log_warn "sweep: a sync holds the lock; nothing swept (try again after it)"
    return "$EX_SKIP"
  fi
  _swl=$(mktemp) || return 1
  crumb_roots "$_swp" | while IFS= read -r _swr; do
    crumbs_in "$_swr" "$_swa"
  done >"$_swl"
  _swn=0; _swb=0; _swf=0
  while IFS= read -r _swc; do
    [ -n "$_swc" ] || continue
    _sws=$(stat -c %s "$_swc" 2>/dev/null) || _sws=0
    if [ -n "$_swd" ]; then
      # log_warn, not log_info: charon-sync pins LOG_LEVEL 2, and a dry run
      # whose whole output is suppressed answers the question with silence.
      log_warn "sweep: WOULD remove $_swc ($_sws bytes)"
      _swn=$((_swn + 1)); _swb=$((_swb + _sws))
    elif rm -f -- "$_swc" 2>/dev/null; then
      log_info "sweep: removed $_swc ($_sws bytes)"
      _swn=$((_swn + 1)); _swb=$((_swb + _sws))
    else
      # Loud, never silent: a crumb charon can SEE and cannot remove is drift a
      # human has to know about (a read-only remote, a permission problem).
      log_error "sweep: could NOT remove $_swc"
      _swf=$((_swf + 1))
    fi
  done <"$_swl"
  rm -f "$_swl"
  flock -u 9 2>/dev/null; exec 9>&-
  if [ "$_swn" -eq 0 ] && [ "$_swf" -eq 0 ]; then
    log_warn "sweep: no orphaned unison temps older than ${_swa}m"
  elif [ -n "$_swd" ]; then
    log_warn "sweep: DRY RUN -- would remove $_swn orphaned unison temp(s),"\
             "$_swb bytes. Nothing was deleted."
  else
    log_info "sweep: removed $_swn orphaned unison temp(s), $_swb bytes"
  fi
  [ "$_swf" -eq 0 ] || return 1
  return 0
}

# The GATE for one profile:  0 go / EX_SKIP skip cheaply / 1 fault (notified).
gate_profile() {   # <profile> <service-name>
  _gs=$(profile_source_name "$1")
  if [ -z "$_gs" ]; then
    notify flag "$2" "profile '$1' names no source"
    return 1
  fi
  _gm=$(source_get "$_gs" MOUNT); _gc=$(source_get "$_gs" CACHE_ROOT)
  if [ -z "$_gm" ] || [ -z "$_gc" ]; then
    notify flag "$2" "profile '$1' names source '$_gs', which is not defined"
    return 1
  fi

  check_runtime_prereqs "$_gs" "$_gc"
  case $? in
    0) : ;;
    2) log_warn "SKIPPED charon-$1: no work done this pass"
       return "$EX_SKIP" ;;
    *) notify flag "$2" \
         "source '$_gs' or its cache is not ready; check the source."
       return 1 ;;
  esac

  # prereqs prove the plumbing is up; this proves the SOURCE is not lying about
  # being empty. A fault, not a skip: an empty source over a populated cache
  # means something is wrong, and silence would be the whole bug again.
  if ! check_source_sane "$_gs" "$_gm" "$_gc" "$(profile_subtree "$1")"; then
    MSG="source '$_gs' for profile '$1' looks dead (empty); pass REFUSED so"
    MSG="$MSG no deletions propagate. Check the source."
    notify flag "$2" "$MSG"
    return 1
  fi
  return 0
}

# Run unison for <unison-profile>, under a sleep inhibitor if one can be had
# and WITHOUT it if not. Returns unison's own status.
#
# unison blocks in uninterruptible D-state on a FUSE mount, so a suspend
# mid-pass fails at the freeze step and (with logind retrying a lid close)
# LOOPS. A sleep BLOCK inhibitor makes a suspend DEFER until the pass ends.
#
# But the inhibitor is a NICETY, never a prerequisite. Acquiring a block
# inhibitor needs polkit authorization that a --user manager session with NO
# SEAT does not get: polkit answers "Access denied ... requires interactive
# authentication" and systemd-inhibit exits 1. The whole pass used to hang off
# that one command, so a denial did not degrade the sync, it CANCELLED it, and
# manifestor failed 5 of 6 timed runs that way while manifold (seated session)
# looked perfectly healthy. Not syncing is strictly worse than syncing
# unprotected, so a denial WARNS and proceeds. The inhibitor is PROBED with a
# throwaway acquire first, so a real non-zero is always unison's, never
# polkit's.
#
# The timeout is a BACKSTOP, not a pacer. At the old 600s it was neither: a
# cold or large pass over FUSE routinely outran it, and every kill stranded a
# partial transfer temp in the destination (see the ignore in prf:charon for
# what that cost). The inhibitor protects suspend; the timeout only has to
# bound a genuinely WEDGED run, so it sits far above any healthy pass.
# Run a command with its combined output BOTH streamed and captured to <file>,
# returning the COMMAND's status rather than tee's. An empty <file> skips the
# capture entirely and runs the command untouched.
#
# STREAMED, NOT BUFFERED: the journal must keep seeing unison's warnings and
# failures as they happen, not in one lump when a pass that may run for an hour
# finally ends. So this is a pipeline, and `$?` after a pipeline is tee's,
# PIPESTATUS is not POSIX, which is why the status is smuggled out through a
# file written INSIDE the pipeline. If the shell is killed before that write,
# the file stays absent and this returns 125, which no unison status can be, so
# a caller cannot mistake "no verdict recorded" for a real one.
run_teed() {   # <file|""> <cmd...>
  _tf=$1; shift
  [ -n "$_tf" ] || { "$@"; return $?; }
  _trc=$_tf.rc
  rm -f "$_trc"
  { "$@" 2>&1; echo $? >"$_trc"; } | tee -- "$_tf"
  _trv=$(cat "$_trc" 2>/dev/null)
  rm -f "$_trc"
  case $_trv in
    ''|*[!0-9]*) return 125 ;;
    *) return "$_trv" ;;
  esac
}

# --- PUSH AUTHORISATION: a node must EARN the right to write the remote -------
#
# THE HAZARD, measured 2026-10-05. Put a stale cache beside a current remote
# with NO unison archive and unison treats every local-only file as NEW and
# pushes it,
# so a file the fleet DELETED months ago returns to the remote at rc=0 with no
# warning. 500 of them is still rc=0: confirmbigdel guards mass DELETION, not
# mass ADDITION. That is the provisioning case, and with three nodes on one
# remote it is the realistic one.
#
# THE FIX IS SAFE-BY-CONSTRUCTION, NOT A GATE. Until a profile is authorised,
# every pass runs with `-nocreation` and `-noupdate` on the MOUNT root, so:
#   nothing is created on the remote   -> no resurrection
#   nothing is updated on the remote   -> no stale content overwriting current
#   remote -> cache still flows        -> the legitimate first seed works
# Verified: the zombie stays local and unpushed, the remote's version wins in
# the cache, and the stale local copy is twinned (locally, per the twin ignore).
#
# WHY NOT PROBE FOR THE ARCHIVE INSTEAD. `unison -showarchive` prints the
# hashcodes and looks perfect for it, and it is a TRAP: it CREATES the archives
# it reports. Measured, it turns a loud dangerous pass into a SILENT one, the
# warning disappears and the resurrection still happens. Do not reach for it.
push_ok_file() { printf '%s/push-ok/%s' "$CHARON_STATE" "$1"; }
push_authorised() { [ -f "$(push_ok_file "$1")" ]; }

# Authorise, recording WHY, because a decision that leaves no artifact is one
# that silently did not happen.
authorise_push() {   # <profile> <reason>
  _apf=$(push_ok_file "$1")
  mkdir -p "${_apf%/*}" || return 1
  printf 'AUTHORISED=%s\nREASON=%s\n' "$(date +%s)" "$2" >"$_apf"
}

# Back to pull-only. `rm -f` on ONE resolved file, never a recursive delete and
# never a glob: the caller has already checked the profile exists, and the only
# thing this may remove is the record named after it.
revoke_push() {   # <profile>
  _rpf=$(push_ok_file "$1")
  [ -f "$_rpf" ] || return 0
  rm -f "$_rpf"
}

# Is it SAFE to authorise without a human looking? Only when there is nothing of
# unknown provenance to push. Two ways that holds:
#   - this UNISON_DIR already has archives, so this node has been syncing and is
#     not a fresh join. THIS IS WHAT AVOIDS A MIGRATION CLIFF: every existing
#     node authorises itself on the next install and notices nothing.
#   - the cache subtree is EMPTY, so there is nothing local to resurrect, the
#     legitimate first seed the dead-source guard already protects.
# What is left is the dangerous case: a non-empty cache on a node with no
# archives at all, which is content charon cannot vouch for.
push_safe_to_authorise() {   # <profile>
  for _psa in "$UNISON_DIR"/ar*; do
    [ -e "$_psa" ] && return 0
    break
  done
  _psr=$(resolve_profile_roots "$1" 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  _pst=${_psr#*|}; _psc=${_pst%%|*}
  dir_has_entries "$_psc" || return 0
  return 1
}

run_unison_guarded() {   # <unison-profile> [capture-file]
  _up=$1
  _ucap=${2:-}
  # Tell unison WHERE the profile is. charon has always had UNISON_DIR for
  # where it WRITES profiles, but never passed it on, so the two agreed only
  # because both default to ~/.unison. Override UNISON_DIR and charon would
  # write a profile unison never reads, then fail with a usage message that
  # names nothing. Export it so the writer and the reader cannot disagree.
  UNISON=$UNISON_DIR; export UNISON
  _inh="systemd-inhibit --what=sleep --who=charon-sync --mode=block"
  set -- timeout --kill-after=30 "${CHARON_SYNC_TIMEOUT:-3600}" \
    unison "$_up" -auto -batch -ui text -silent
    # PULL-ONLY until authorised. Passed on the COMMAND LINE rather than written
    # into the prf, because it is a property of this NODE's history, not of the
    # profile, and baking it in would make the prf differ between nodes and so
    # fail check's drift diff on every box but one.
    if [ -n "${CHARON_PULL_ONLY_ROOT:-}" ]; then
      set -- "$@" -nocreation "$CHARON_PULL_ONLY_ROOT" \
                  -noupdate "$CHARON_PULL_ONLY_ROOT"
    fi
  if $_inh --why="charon-sync probe" true 2>/dev/null; then
    run_teed "$_ucap" $_inh \
      --why="unison $_up syncing (unfreezable on FUSE)" "$@"
    return $?
  fi
  log_warn "cannot hold the sleep inhibitor (polkit denied, or"\
           "systemd-inhibit absent); syncing UNPROTECTED -- a suspend"\
           "mid-pass may wedge on the FUSE mount"
  run_teed "$_ucap" "$@"
}

run_sync_profile() {
  PROFILE="$1"
  UNISON_PROFILE="charon-$PROFILE"
  SERVICE_NAME="charon-${PROFILE}-sync"

  gate_profile "$PROFILE" "$SERVICE_NAME"; _g=$?
  [ "$_g" -eq 0 ] || return "$_g"

  # PULL-ONLY until this profile is authorised to write the remote. Announced at
  # WARN, not INFO, because charon-sync pins LOG_LEVEL=2 and a pass that is
  # deliberately refusing to push must not be able to do so silently.
  CHARON_PULL_ONLY_ROOT=
  if ! push_authorised "$PROFILE"; then
    _rsp=$(resolve_profile_roots "$PROFILE" 2>/dev/null) && {
      CHARON_PULL_ONLY_ROOT=${_rsp%%|*}
      log_warn "profile '$PROFILE' is PULL-ONLY: this node has not been"\
               "authorised to write the remote, so nothing will be created or"\
               "updated there. Review '${APP_NAME%% *} plan $PROFILE', then"\
               "'${APP_NAME%% *} allow-push $PROFILE'."
    }
  fi
  export CHARON_PULL_ONLY_ROOT
  log_info "running Unison profile (non-interactive): $UNISON_PROFILE"
  # Capture unison's output so the failed PATHS can be recorded. A capture that
  # cannot be created is not a reason to skip the pass: syncing matters, knowing
  # which paths failed is a bonus, so this degrades to the old behaviour.
  CAPTURE=$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/charon-uni-$PROFILE.XXXXXX" 2>/dev/null) \
    || CAPTURE=
  PASS_NAMED_A_PATH=
  run_unison_guarded "$UNISON_PROFILE" "$CAPTURE"; RET=$?
  if [ -n "$CAPTURE" ]; then
    # SETTLED FIRST, because it is a diff against the record that
    # record_failures is about to overwrite. Only from a pass whose list is
    # complete: treating a truncated list as "these paths are fine now" is what
    # would delete a temp a resume still needs.
    if unison_rc_reported "$RET"; then
      NEWSET=$CAPTURE.set
      parse_unison_failures "$CAPTURE" >"$NEWSET" 2>/dev/null || : >"$NEWSET"
      # THIS pass's answer, kept for the pull-only decision below. It cannot be
      # re-read from the record afterwards: record_failures deliberately LEAVES
      # a previous record alone when a failing pass parses nothing, so reading
      # it back would answer about an older pass.
      [ -s "$NEWSET" ] && PASS_NAMED_A_PATH=y
      sweep_settled "$PROFILE" "$(settled_paths "$PROFILE" "$NEWSET")"
      rm -f "$NEWSET"
    fi
    record_failures "$PROFILE" "$RET" "$CAPTURE"
    rm -f "$CAPTURE"
    # AFTER recording, so the streak this reads is the one this pass just made.
    # A profile that keeps failing the identical way is the fault nothing could
    # clear until now; see unwedge_profile for why dropping the temps is the one
    # safe intervention available.
    unwedge_profile "$PROFILE"
  fi

  if [ $RET -eq 0 ]; then
    # A PULL-ONLY PASS THAT WITHHELD NOTHING AUTHORISES ITSELF, which narrows
    # the human gate to the only case that needs one: a cache that really does
    # hold content the remote has not got. A cache restored from a recent
    # backup, or copied from a node that was in step, has nothing to vouch for,
    # and making a human confirm that is the kind of nag that gets disabled.
    #
    # THE EVIDENCE IS THE EXIT CODE, AND IT WAS MEASURED BEFORE BEING TRUSTED
    # (2026-10-05, eight cases against real unison). A pull-only pass that
    # withheld ANY write exits 1 with a `<=?=>` row: a create, an update, a
    # delete, an mtime-only change, and a withheld push riding alongside a
    # successful pull, which does NOT mask the skip. The three cases that exit 0
    # (identical replicas, a pure pull, a conflict resolved toward the mount)
    # wrote nothing to the remote and withheld nothing. So rc 0 here means the
    # guard had nothing to do.
    #
    # THE FIRST ATTEMPT AT THAT MEASUREMENT SAID THE OPPOSITE and was wrong in a
    # way worth remembering: its fixture wrote "v1" then "v2", the same SIZE in
    # the same second, and `fastcheck` compares mtime and size, so unison never
    # saw the change at all and reported "replicas have not changed". That read
    # as a write withheld silently at rc 0. Change the LENGTH and sleep past the
    # second boundary, or the reconciler is given nothing to find.
    #
    # fastcheck CANNOT hide anything in the case this gate exists for, because
    # that case is a cache with NO archive: there is no previous record to take
    # a shortcut against, so every cache-only path is seen as new.
    if [ -n "$CHARON_PULL_ONLY_ROOT" ]; then
      authorise_push "$PROFILE" "pull-only pass withheld nothing" \
        && log_warn "profile '$PROFILE': the pull-only pass withheld NOTHING,"\
                    "so this node is now authorised to write the remote."\
                    "Undo with '${APP_NAME%% *} allow-push --revoke $PROFILE'."
    fi
    notify clear "$SERVICE_NAME"; return 0
  fi
  # A PULL-ONLY PASS REPORTS 1 WHEN IT REFUSED A PUSH, and that is the guard
  # working, not a failure. Measured 2026-10-05: nocreation/noupdate on the
  # mount root makes unison SKIP those items, printing a `<=?=>` row per item,
  # and exit 1.
  #
  # THREE CONDITIONS, and every one of them is load-bearing. A profile can stay
  # unauthorised indefinitely, because only a human can clear it, so anything
  # forgiven here is forgiven for as long as that lasts:
  #   - pull-only, or this is an ordinary fault;
  #   - rc exactly 1, since 2 and above are propagation failures the guard
  #     cannot explain;
  #   - and charon NAMED NO FAILING PATH. This is the sharp one. A genuine SCAN
  #     failure ALSO exits 1 (measured: an unreadable directory), so rc alone
  #     cannot tell the two apart, and a blanket rc-1 forgiveness would swallow
  #     it. It can be told apart by the output: a scan failure emits the `error`
  #     column row parse_unison_failures anchors on, and the guard's skipped
  #     items emit `<=?=>` rows, which it ignores by design.
  if [ -n "$CHARON_PULL_ONLY_ROOT" ] && [ "$RET" -eq 1 ] \
     && [ -z "$PASS_NAMED_A_PATH" ]; then
    log_warn "profile '$PROFILE': pull-only pass complete; unison reported"\
             "skipped items (exit 1), which is what refusing to write the"\
             "remote looks like. The cache is up to date with the remote."
    notify clear "$SERVICE_NAME"; return 0
  fi
  MSG="Unison $UNISON_PROFILE failed (exit $RET)."
  # NAME THE PATHS IN THE FLAG ITSELF. The flag is often the only thing a human
  # ever reads about a fault, and "exit 2, go read the log" made them go and
  # read the log, which on 2026-09-24 meant grepping the journal by hand for
  # six paths unison had already printed plainly.
  _rsf=$(failed_summary "$PROFILE")
  if [ -n "$_rsf" ]; then
    MSG="$MSG $_rsf, e.g. $(failed_paths "$PROFILE" | head -1)."
  fi
  MSG="$MSG Run it interactively and"
  MSG="$MSG check ~/.unison/unison.log."
  # ALWAYS log, THEN also notify. The old form logged only when NO notifier was
  # configured, which made a set-but-missing notifier swallow the fault whole:
  # notify() no-ops on an absent command and nothing else printed, so the
  # journal recorded a bare "exit 1" with no reason. A fault is loud now
  # regardless of whether anyone is listening on the notify seam.
  log_error "$MSG"
  notify flag "$SERVICE_NAME" "$MSG"
  return $RET
}

# The worse of two outcomes, by SEVERITY rather than recency: fault > skip >
# success. `sync all` must report the worst thing that happened, or a fault
# hides behind a later profile's success. A named function so the unit test
# exercises THIS logic rather than a copy of it, because a test of a duplicate
# can
# pass while the real code is wrong.
worse_of() {   # <a> <b>
  case "$2" in
    0) printf '%s' "$1" ;;
    "$EX_SKIP") [ "$1" -eq 0 ] && printf '%s' "$EX_SKIP" || printf '%s' "$1" ;;
    *) printf '%s' "$2" ;;
  esac
}

run_with_lock() {
  PROFILE="$1"   # a profile name, or "all"
  mkdir -p "$LOCKDIR"
  exec 9>"$LOCKFILE"
  if ! flock -n 9 2>/dev/null; then
    log_warn "SKIPPED '$PROFILE': another charon sync holds the lock"
    return "$EX_SKIP"
  fi
  if [ "$PROFILE" != all ]; then
    run_sync_profile "$PROFILE"; return $?
  fi
  _rc=0
  for p in $(profile_names); do
    run_sync_profile "$p"; _rc=$(worse_of "$_rc" "$?")
  done
  return "$_rc"
}

#### higher-level operations ####
# What is true right now, for a human, at a glance. Distinct from `check`,
# which is a machine verdict: status never fails, never probes, never writes,
# and answers "is my sync working, and what is it about to do". It reads only
# systemd state and config, so it is cheap enough to run reflexively.
do_status() {
  _now=$(date +%s)
  _any=
  for _s in $(source_names); do
    _sm=$(source_get "$_s" MOUNT); _sc=$(source_get "$_s" CACHE_ROOT)
    [ -n "$_sm" ] || { echo "source '$_s'  NOT DEFINED"; _any=1; continue; }
    if "$CHARON_LIBEXEC/charon-source" status "$_s" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      _live=live
    else
      _live="DOWN"
    fi
    _prov=$(source_get "$_s" PROVIDER); _prov=${_prov:-none}
    printf 'source %-12s %-7s %s -> %s  [%s]\n' \
      "'$_s'" "$_prov" "$_sm" "$_sc" "$_live"
    if traits_present "$_s"; then
      _tp=$(traits_get "$_s" PROBED)
      printf '  traits: %s, %s, mtimes %s   measured %s\n' \
        "$(traits_get "$_s" FSTYPE)" \
        "case-$(traits_get "$_s" CASE)" \
        "$(traits_get "$_s" TIMES)" "${_tp%T*}"
    else
      printf '  traits: NONE; run: charon source probe %s\n' "$_s"
    fi

    for _p in $(profile_names); do
      [ "$(profile_source_name "$_p")" = "$_s" ] || continue
      _any=1
      _sub=$(profile_subtree "$_p")
      _iv=$(profile_get "$_p" INTERVAL); _iv=${_iv:-30m}
      printf '\n  profile %-12s %s  every %s, next %s\n' \
        "'$_p'" "$_s:${_sub:-<whole source>}" "$_iv" "$(profile_next_run "$_p")"
      _oc=$(profile_outcome "$_p")
      _tok=${_oc%%|*}; _rest=${_oc#*|}
      _ep=${_rest%%|*}; _det=${_rest#*|}
      case "$_tok" in
        never) printf '    last run: never\n' ;;
        ok)    printf '    last run: OK, %s\n' "$(human_age "$_ep" "$_now")" ;;
        skip)  printf '    last run: SKIPPED %s, %s\n' \
                 "($_det)" "$(human_age "$_ep" "$_now")" ;;
        *)     printf '    last run: FAILED (%s), %s\n' \
                 "$_det" "$(human_age "$_ep" "$_now")" ;;
      esac
      _fsum=$(failed_summary "$_p" "$_now")
      if [ -n "$_fsum" ]; then
        printf '    failing:  %s\n' "$_fsum"
        failed_paths "$_p" | head -3 | sed 's/^/                /'
        _fn=$(failed_paths "$_p" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
        [ "$_fn" -gt 3 ] && printf '                ... and %s more\n' \
          "$((_fn - 3))"
      fi
      _cf=$(profile_get "$_p" CONFLICT); _dl=$(profile_get "$_p" DELETE)
      _ig=$(profile_get_all "$_p" IGNORE | tr '\n' ' ')
      printf '    policy:   conflict=%s delete=%s%s\n' \
        "${_cf:-remote}" "${_dl:-propagate}" \
        "$([ -n "$_ig" ] && printf ' ignore=%s' "$_ig")"
      # WHETHER THIS NODE PUSHES AT ALL, which is the most consequential thing
      # about a profile and was the one thing status did not say. A pull-only
      # node syncs happily, reports every pass successful and never propagates a
      # local change, so a human reading "last run: OK" would have no way to
      # know. Shouted, because it is the unusual state.
      if push_authorised "$_p"; then
        printf '    push:     allowed, since %s\n' \
          "$(human_age "$(sed -n 's/^AUTHORISED=//p' "$(push_ok_file "$_p")" \
               | head -1)" "$(date +%s)")"
      else
        printf '    push:     PULL-ONLY, this node will not write the remote\n'
        printf '              review: %s plan %s\n' "${APP_NAME%% *}" "$_p"
        printf '              allow:  %s allow-push %s\n' \
          "${APP_NAME%% *}" "$_p"
      fi
      printf '    artifacts: %s\n' "$UNISON_DIR/charon-$_p.prf"
    done
    echo
  done
  [ -n "$_any" ] || echo "no profiles configured ($PROFILES_DIR)"
}

# `config` was the old name; it only ever printed where things live.

do_install() {
  log_info "install: commencing"
  check_install_prereqs
  install_packages
  if [ -z "$(profile_names)" ]; then no_profiles_hint; fi
  if ! check_config_sanity >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    log_error "refusing to install from an incoherent config:"
    check_config_sanity | sed 's/^/  /' >&2
    exit 2
  fi
  ensure_source_traits
  ensure_cache_roots
  ensure_push_authorisation
  setup_unison_profiles
  # A unit that could not be RENDERED is not something to report success over:
  # the existing one was deliberately kept, so the box is running the previous
  # version's artifact and check will say so. Loud, and non-zero.
  if ! setup_systemd_units; then
    log_error "install: one or more generated artifacts could not be written;"\
              "the previous ones were KEPT. Run '${APP_NAME%% *} check'."
    return 1
  fi

  # Seed the priority subtrees SYNCHRONOUSLY (a fresh box needs them now, e.g. a
  # desktop wallpaper), then bulk-seed the full configured tree in BACKGROUND
  # (fast/parallel/resumable rclone copy, not a cold unison-over-FUSE crawl).
  # shellcheck disable=SC2046
  seed_priority || true
  if command -v systemd-run >/dev/null 2>&1 \
     && systemd-run --user --collect --unit=charon-seed-initial \
          "$_self" seed-full >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    log_info "install: full bulk seed launched (unit charon-seed-initial)"
  else
    setsid "$_self" seed-full </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &
    log_info "install: full bulk seed launched (detached)"
  fi
  log_info "install: complete"
}

# Each check_* below prints its own [OK]/[WARN]/[FAIL] lines and returns
# non-zero on DRIFT; do_check only aggregates. Split out because one function
# was doing four unrelated audits.
check_service_template() {
  _r=0
  # DIFF it, do not merely look for it. Re-render with the notify path the unit
  # itself carries: that value is install-time INTEGRATOR policy and cannot be
  # re-derived from charon's own config, so assuming this shell's CHARON_NOTIFY
  # would report a correct unit as drift. Whether that baked path actually
  # resolves is audited separately, by check_notify_seam.
  _bn=$(sed -n 's/^Environment=CHARON_NOTIFY=//p' \
          "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sync@.service" 2>/dev/null | head -1)
  _rt=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/charon-unit-render.$$
  render_sync_unit "$_bn" >"$_rt"
  check_generated "charon-sync@.service" \
    "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sync@.service" "$_rt" || _r=1
  rm -f "$_rt"
  if systemctl --user list-unit-files 2>/dev/null \
       | grep -q '^charon-sync@\.service'; then
    echo "  [OK]   charon-sync@.service registered"
  else
    fail_drift "charon-sync@.service not registered"; _r=1
  fi
  check_sweep_units || _r=1
  return $_r
}

# The daily sweep pair, diffed like every other generated artifact. A generated
# file nothing compares is invisible drift, and that lesson has now been learned
# here three times (the prf, then the mount unit, then the sync unit).
check_sweep_units() {
  _csr=0
  # SWEEP=off IS A VALID STATE AND MUST CHECK CLEAN. Reporting a user's
  # deliberate choice as drift is worse than a nag: an integrator turns a
  # non-zero check into drift and runs apply, apply re-enables the timer, and
  # the choice is silently reverted on every provision sweep, a fight the user
  # cannot win. So the declaration is asserted in BOTH directions, and what
  # would be drift here is the artifacts SURVIVING the switch to off.
  if ! sweep_scheduled; then
    for _cso in charon-sweep.timer charon-sweep.service; do
      if [ -e "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/$_cso" ]; then
        fail_drift "SWEEP=off but $_cso is still installed; it would keep"\
             "deleting from the remote (run '${APP_NAME} install')"
        _csr=1
      fi
    done
    if have_systemd \
       && systemctl --user is-enabled charon-sweep.timer >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      fail_drift "SWEEP=off but charon-sweep.timer is still ENABLED"
      _csr=1
    fi
    [ "$_csr" -ne 0 ] \
      || echo "  [OK]   sweep disabled by config (SWEEP=off); no timer, and"\
              "'${APP_NAME%% *} sweep' still works by hand"
    return $_csr
  fi
  _crt=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/charon-sweep-render.$$
  render_sweep_unit >"$_crt"
  check_generated "charon-sweep.service" \
    "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sweep.service" "$_crt" || _csr=1
  render_sweep_timer >"$_crt"
  check_generated "charon-sweep.timer" \
    "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sweep.timer" "$_crt" || _csr=1
  rm -f "$_crt"
  # Gate on the EXIT STATUS, as every other check here does. Comparing
  # is-enabled's stdout to "enabled" looks tidier and is wrong: it makes the
  # assertion depend on a tool's output text rather than its verdict, and it
  # cannot pass against the suite's stubs, which model the verdict.
  if systemctl --user is-enabled charon-sweep.timer >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "  [OK]   charon-sweep.timer enabled ($(sweep_mode))"
  else
    fail_drift "charon-sweep.timer not enabled: orphaned Unison temps will"\
         "accumulate unswept (run '${APP_NAME} install')"
    _csr=1
  fi
  return $_csr
}

# Three questions per profile, because any one of them alone lies: is the timer
# ARMED, did the last run actually WORK, and is the live prf the one THIS
# version would generate.
# Is the timer ARMED, and is it the one this version would write.
check_profile_timer() {   # <profile>
  _ptr=0
  _t="charon-sync-$1.timer"
  if systemctl --user is-enabled "$_t" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "  [OK]   $_t enabled"
  else fail_drift "$_t not enabled"; _ptr=1; fi
  if systemctl --user is-active "$_t" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "  [OK]   $_t active"
  else echo "  [WARN] $_t not active"; fi
  _tt=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/charon-timer-render.$$.$1
  render_timer "$1" >"$_tt"
  check_generated "$_t" "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/$_t" "$_tt" || _ptr=1
  rm -f "$_tt"
  return $_ptr
}

# Is this profile ordered after the mount of the source it actually draws on.
# An ABSENT drop-in is not automatically drift: a PROVIDER=none profile
# correctly has none, and reporting that as missing would train the reader to
# ignore the line. Assert the state that SHOULD hold, either way.
check_profile_ordering() {   # <profile>
  _por=0
  _dw=$(render_sync_dropin "$1")
  _df=$(sync_dropin_file "$1")
  if [ -z "$_dw" ]; then
    if [ -f "$_df" ]; then
      fail_drift "$1 has an ordering drop-in but draws on no charon-mounted"\
           "source; run '${APP_NAME} install'"
      _por=1
    else
      echo "  [OK]   $1 needs no mount ordering (source is not charon-mounted)"
    fi
  else
    _dt=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/charon-dropin-render.$$.$1
    printf '%s\n' "$_dw" >"$_dt"
    check_generated "charon-sync@$1 ordering drop-in" "$_df" "$_dt" || _por=1
    rm -f "$_dt"
  fi
  return $_por
}

# PRESENCE IS NOT FUNCTION. An enabled, active timer whose service fails every
# single pass looks perfectly healthy to a presence check, which is how
# manifestor sat at 5-of-6 failed runs behind an all-[OK] audit for hours. The
# outcome comes from profile_outcome, shared with status, so the two cannot
# disagree about what the same run meant.
check_profile_outcome() {   # <profile>
  _u="charon-sync@$1.service"
  _oc=$(profile_outcome "$1")
  case "${_oc%%|*}" in
    never) echo "  [WARN] $_u has not run yet" ;;
    ok)    echo "  [OK]   $_u last run: success" ;;
    skip)  echo "  [WARN] $_u last run: SKIPPED, $(printf '%s' "$_oc" |
                  cut -d'|' -f3)" ;;
    *)     fail_fault "$_u last run: $(printf '%s' "$_oc" | cut -d'|' -f3)"
           report_failed_paths "$1"
           return 1 ;;
  esac
  # A profile can be SUCCEEDING overall and still have paths unison could not
  # propagate, because unison exits 2 only for a transfer failure. Say so, and
  # make it a FAULT rather than a footnote: a green [OK] line above a quietly
  # stuck path is the "presence is not function" trap in a new place, and until
  # now this printed the paths while still returning success.
  if [ -n "$(failed_paths "$1")" ]; then
    fail_fault "$_u last run SUCCEEDED but paths are still failing"
    report_failed_paths "$1"
    return 1
  fi
  return 0
}

# The paths under a verdict line, indented, truncated, and ending in an
# explicit `return 0` so a false trailing test cannot become this function's
# status (that exact shape once aborted an apply under set -e).
report_failed_paths() {   # <profile>
  _rpp=$(failed_paths "$1")
  [ -n "$_rpp" ] || return 0
  echo "         $(failed_summary "$1"):"
  printf '%s\n' "$_rpp" | head -5 | sed 's/^/           /'
  _rpc=$(printf '%s\n' "$_rpp" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
  if [ "$_rpc" -gt 5 ]; then
    echo "           ... and $((_rpc - 5)) more"
  fi
  return 0
}

# Render to a TEMP FILE, not a pipe: the comparison stays byte-exact, and a
# render FAILURE becomes distinguishable from a mismatch. Reporting an
# unresolvable source as "hand-patched, or a stale install" sends the reader
# hunting for an edit that does not exist, exactly what happened
# mid-migration, when profiles named a source not published yet.
check_profile_prf() {   # <profile>
  _ppr=0
  _prf=$UNISON_DIR/charon-$1.prf
  _rt=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/charon-render.$$.$1
  if [ ! -f "$_prf" ]; then
    fail_drift "$_prf missing (run '${APP_NAME} install')"; _ppr=1
  elif ! render_prf "$1" >"$_rt" 2>"$_rt.err"; then
    fail_fault "charon-$1.prf cannot be RE-RENDERED, so it cannot be"\
         "checked. The profile or its source is unresolvable:"
    sed 's/^/           /' "$_rt.err" | head -3
    _ppr=1
  elif diff -q "$_rt" "$_prf" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "  [OK]   charon-$1.prf matches this version's template"
  else
    fail_drift "charon-$1.prf DIFFERS from this version's template"\
         "(hand-patched, or a stale install); run '${APP_NAME} install'"
    _ppr=1
  fi
  rm -f "$_rt" "$_rt.err"
  return $_ppr
}

# FOUR independent questions, because any one of them alone lies: is the timer
# armed, is it ordered behind its own source's mount, did the last run actually
# WORK, and is the live prf the one this version would generate. Each reports
# its own markers; this only aggregates, and deliberately runs ALL of them
# rather than short-circuiting, so one failure never hides the next.
check_profile_health() {   # <profile>
  _phr=0
  check_profile_timer    "$1" || _phr=1
  check_profile_ordering "$1" || _phr=1
  check_profile_outcome  "$1" || _phr=1
  check_profile_prf      "$1" || _phr=1
  return $_phr
}

# Orphaned unison temps are LITTER, and litter is deliberately NOT drift: this
# reports and returns 0. Making it fail would put `charon check` into exactly
# the trap the 2026-09-24 incident exposed: an integrator turns a non-zero
# check into drift, runs apply, and apply has no verb that clears it, so the
# provision loop spins forever on something harmless. The remedy is a SEPARATE
# verb a human (or a cron) invokes, and check's job is only to stop the litter
# being invisible.
# It scans the MOUNT, which costs a few seconds through FUSE. That is the price
# of an honest answer: the crumbs that matter are the ones on the remote, and a
# cache-only scan would have reported zero all through the incident.
# A TWIN IS A BACKUP, NOT LITTER, so this REPORTS and deliberately calls it
# neither drift nor fault. Same reasoning the crumb scan already carries: an
# integrator turns a non-zero check into drift and runs apply, apply has no
# verb that deletes a user's backup, and the provision loop then spins forever
# on something that is working as designed. charon will not delete these.
#
# THE TWO SIDES MEAN OPPOSITE THINGS:
#   CACHE  expected. The twin is this box's copy of what it was about to lose,
#          and keeping it local is the whole point of the ignore.
#   MOUNT  the incidental remote write the ignore exists to prevent. Post-fix
#          it means another node has not taken this version, or the ignore
#          regressed. Worth saying loudly; still not something apply can fix.
check_twins() {
  # shellcheck disable=SC2046  # four integers, splitting is the intent
  set -- $(twin_tally)
  if [ "${1:-0}" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "  [WARN] $1 unison conflict copy/copies ON THE REMOTE"\
         "(${2:-0} bytes). charon ignores these now, so they will not sync"\
         "or be deleted: another node may predate that, or the ignore has"\
         "regressed. Remove them from the remote once you have decided"\
         "which copy you want."
  fi
  if [ "${3:-0}" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo "  [WARN] $3 unison conflict copy/copies in the cache"\
         "(${4:-0} bytes), kept LOCAL by design. Each one is a version this"\
         "box was about to overwrite; charon will not remove them."
  fi
  return 0
}

# The subtrees every profile of <source> reconciles, space separated. The audit
# needs these and charon-source cannot read profiles (that is this half's job),
# so the plumbing goes through an env var rather than teaching the provider
# about profile config.
source_subtrees() {   # <source>
  for _ssp in $(profile_names); do
    [ "$(profile_source_name "$_ssp")" = "$1" ] || continue
    _sst=$(profile_subtree "$_ssp"); [ -n "$_sst" ] && printf '%s\n' "$_sst"
  done | sort -u | tr '\n' ' '
}

# Audit each rclone-backed source's remote. Reports; the verdict is the worst of
# the sources, and a FAULT because charon cannot collapse a duplicate itself.
do_audit() {   # [source]
  _aurc=0; _auany=
  for _aus in ${1:-$(source_names)}; do
    # AN UNKNOWN NAME MUST BE REFUSED NON-ZERO. charon-source's own gate prints
    # the refusal, but its exit was being swallowed here: the substitution below
    # failed, the loop carried on, and the function returned 0, so `charon audit
    # nosuch` printed an ERROR and reported success. A refusal that exits 0 is
    # the shape an integrator reads as "all clear".
    if ! _aup=$("$CHARON_LIBEXEC/charon-source" provider "$_aus"); then
      return 2
    fi
    [ "$_aup" = rclone ] || continue
    _auany=y
    CHARON_AUDIT_SUBTREES="$(source_subtrees "$_aus")" \
      "$CHARON_LIBEXEC/charon-source" audit "$_aus" \
      || _aurc=$(worse_of "$_aurc" "$?")
  done
  [ -n "$_auany" ] \
    || log_info "audit: no rclone-backed source; nothing to audit"
  return "$_aurc"
}

# Read what the LAST audit found. Free: no remote call, the same shape as
# reading a unit's last-run result rather than re-running the unit.
#
# A duplicate name on the remote is a FAULT, not drift: it manufactures
# conflicts on every node and charon cannot collapse it (that means choosing
# which object survives, and on a file this account does not own the API
# refuses outright). Never audited is a WARN, because an absent measurement is
# not evidence of a problem and an install cannot create one either.
# PULL-ONLY is a WARN, never drift or fault. It is charon working as designed on
# a node whose cache provenance is unknown, and the only thing that can clear it
# is a HUMAN deciding, so a verdict would make an integrator schedule an apply
# that cannot help. Same reasoning as the crumb and twin reports.
check_push_authorisation() {
  for _cpp in $(profile_names); do
    push_authorised "$_cpp" && continue
    echo "  [WARN] profile '$_cpp' is PULL-ONLY: charon will not create or"\
         "update anything on the remote for it. Review"\
         "'${APP_NAME%% *} plan $_cpp', then '${APP_NAME%% *} allow-push"\
         "$_cpp'."
  done
  return 0
}

check_audit() {
  _carc=0; _caany=
  for _cas in $(source_names); do
    _cap=$("$CHARON_LIBEXEC/charon-source" provider "$_cas")
    [ "$_cap" = rclone ] || continue
    _caany=y
    _caf="$CHARON_STATE/audit/$_cas"
    if [ ! -f "$_caf" ]; then
      echo "  [WARN] source '$_cas': the remote has never been audited for"\
           "duplicate names (run '${APP_NAME%% *} audit')"
      continue
    fi
    # COUNT the detail lines; the record carries no summary to disagree with.
    _cad=$(grep -c '^DUPE=' "$_caf" 2>/dev/null) || _cad=0
    _can=$(grep -c '^NOCONTENT=' "$_caf" 2>/dev/null) || _can=0
    _cat=$(sed -n 's/^AUDITED=//p' "$_caf" | head -1)
    if [ "$_cad" = 0 ] && [ "$_can" = 0 ]; then
      echo "  [OK]   source '$_cas': remote had no duplicate names when last"\
           "audited $(human_age "${_cat:-0}" "$(date +%s)")"
      continue
    fi
    fail_fault "source '$_cas': the remote had $_cad duplicated path(s) and"\
         "$_can contentless object(s) when last audited"\
         "($(human_age "${_cat:-0}" "$(date +%s)")). A duplicate name"\
         "makes"\
         "the mount non-deterministic and manufactures conflicts on EVERY"\
         "node. charon cannot collapse it: run"\
         "'rclone dedupe --dedupe-mode largest <remote>:<subtree>'."
    _carc=1
  done
  [ -n "$_caany" ] || return 0
  return "$_carc"
}

# THE PLAN, with NO writes: what the next pass WOULD do.
#
# unison 2.53 has no -dryrun, so the mechanism is REFUSING EVERY WRITE rather
# than declining to confirm one: -nocreation, -noupdate and -nodeletion on BOTH
# roots. unison then reconciles, reports each pending item with its direction,
# and skips it, naming the flag that stopped it. Nothing can be written whatever
# it is told, which is a property of the command line rather than of the
# interaction.
#
# THE OBVIOUS MECHANISM IS THE BROKEN ONE, and it was built first: defeat
# `batch` so unison asks instead of acting, and answer with EOF from /dev/null.
# It works only when an archive exists. With NO archive unison emits its "no
# archive files were found" warning, ends it with `Press return to continue.`,
# consumes the EOF there and exits BEFORE reconciling, so the plan is empty.
# That is the one state where plan matters most: a node with no archive is
# precisely the one held pull-only and told to review this.
#
# AND IT CREATES THE ARCHIVES IT REPORTS ON, which is the `-showarchive` trap in
# a new place, and here it would be self-defeating: an archive left behind by
# plan makes push_safe_to_authorise read this node as "already syncing", so
# merely LOOKING would silently authorise it. So the whole thing runs against a
# THROWAWAY COPY of UNISON_DIR, and the real one is never opened for writing.
#
# IT TAKES THE SYNC LOCK, which is the reason this is a verb rather than a note
# telling you to run unison by hand: the note had to warn you to stop the timer
# first, because a hand-run unison bypasses the lock and can collide with a
# scheduled pass. A verb cannot forget.
do_plan() {   # [profile]
  _plp=${1:-}
  if [ -n "$_plp" ] && [ ! -f "$PROFILES_DIR/$_plp.conf" ]; then
    log_error "unknown profile '$_plp'"; return 2
  fi
  # The lock is held against a concurrent PASS, which is what would make the
  # plan untrue by the time it is read, and is also why running unison by hand
  # was a footgun worth replacing with a verb.
  mkdir -p "$LOCKDIR"
  exec 9>"$LOCKFILE"
  if ! flock -n 9 2>/dev/null; then
    log_warn "plan: a charon sync holds the lock; its result would be stale"\
             "before you read it. Try again when the pass finishes."
    return "$EX_SKIP"
  fi
  # The scratch UNISON dir. ONE for the whole run, so the archives unison writes
  # for the first profile are available to the second, exactly as they would be
  # in the real dir.
  _plt=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/charon-plan.XXXXXX") || {
    log_error "plan: could not make a scratch directory"; return 1; }
  cp -a "$UNISON_DIR"/ar* "$_plt/" 2>/dev/null || :
  UNISON=$_plt; export UNISON
  _plrc=0
  for _pl in ${_plp:-$(profile_names)}; do
    echo "== plan for '$_pl' (NOTHING is written) =="
    if push_authorised "$_pl"; then
      echo "   authorised: a pass MAY create and update on the remote"
    else
      echo "   PULL-ONLY: a pass will refuse to create or update on the remote"
    fi
    if ! cp -a "$UNISON_DIR/charon-$_pl.prf" "$_plt/" 2>/dev/null; then
      echo "   no generated profile; run '${APP_NAME%% *} install'"
      _plrc=1
      continue
    fi
    cp -a "$UNISON_DIR/charon-$_pl.prf.local" "$_plt/" 2>/dev/null || :
    # Resolve the roots so the refusals can name them. A plan for a profile
    # whose roots will not resolve is not a plan, and saying so beats a unison
    # usage message that names nothing.
    if ! _plr=$(resolve_profile_roots "$_pl" 2>&1); then
      echo "   cannot resolve this profile's roots: $_plr"
      _plrc=1
      continue
    fi
    _plm=${_plr%%|*}; _plrest=${_plr#*|}; _plc=${_plrest%%|*}
    # EVERY write refused on BOTH roots, so this cannot propagate anything
    # whatever unison decides. The boilerplate of the no-archive warning is
    # dropped and its first line kept: that line is the heart of why a node is
    # held pull-only; the paragraph under it is 18 lines of advice about DHCP.
    # `-terse` drops the scan PROGRESS display, which is not cosmetic: that
    # line ends in \r, so on a real tree it shared an output line with the next
    # message and the report read
    #   "\ Finance/Some File.pdf      Reconciling changes"
    # The \r squeeze stays alongside it, since -terse governs the messages
    # unison CHOOSES to print and not what a future version redraws.
    #
    # The no-archive warning SURVIVES -terse, which is wanted: its first line
    # is the heart of why a node is held pull-only. Only the boilerplate under
    # it is dropped, 18 lines of advice about DHCP and archive formats.
    #
    # BUT -terse ALSO SWALLOWS "Nothing to do", so a profile in step produced
    # an EMPTY report, which is indistinguishable from a plan that failed to
    # run. charon says that itself rather than depending on unison's wording,
    # which is the honest fix: the alternative was dropping -terse and
    # pattern-matching the progress fragments out, and guessing at a redraw's
    # shape is how a filter silently eats a real line.
    unison "charon-$_pl" -ui text -batch -auto -silent=false -terse -dumbtty \
      -nocreation "$_plm" -noupdate "$_plm" -nodeletion "$_plm" \
      -nocreation "$_plc" -noupdate "$_plc" -nodeletion "$_plc" \
      </dev/null 2>&1 \
      | tr '\r' '\n' \
      | sed -n '/^This can happen either/,/^environment variable/d; p' \
      | grep -vE '^$|^Contacting|^Looking for changes$|^Reconciling changes$' \
      | sed 's/^/   /' >"$_plt/report"
    if [ -s "$_plt/report" ]; then
      cat "$_plt/report"
    else
      echo "   nothing pending: this profile is in step with its source"
    fi
  done
  flock -u 9 2>/dev/null; exec 9>&-
  # The literal, not the variable: a delete whose target is expanded at run time
  # is the shape that removed a working tree in this fleet once. mktemp -d
  # answering empty would make this `rm -rf /ar*`.
  case $_plt in
    "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}"/charon-plan.??????) rm -rf "$_plt" ;;
    *) log_warn "plan: refusing to remove an unexpected scratch dir '$_plt'" ;;
  esac
  [ "$_plrc" = 0 ] || return "$_plrc"
  return 0
}

check_crumbs() {
  # An age charon REFUSES to act on is drift a human must fix, not litter to
  # shrug at: with a bad CRUMB_AGE_MIN nothing is ever swept, silently.
  _cca=$(crumb_age_min) || {
    fail_fault "CRUMB_AGE_MIN is not a whole number of minutes; nothing"\
         "will ever be swept (fix $CHARON_CONFIG_FILE)"
    return 1; }
  # crumb_tally returns "<count> <bytes>" on one line; splitting it into two
  # integers is the intent, not an oversight.
  # shellcheck disable=SC2046
  set -- $(crumb_tally)
  [ "${1:-0}" -gt 0 ] || return 0
  echo "  [WARN] $1 orphaned unison temp(s) on the configured trees"\
       "(${2:-0} bytes), all older than ${_cca}m:"\
       "run '${APP_NAME%% *} sweep'"
  return 0
}

# Audit the notify seam AS BAKED INTO THE UNIT, probed with the UNIT's PATH and
# not this shell's. Checking $CHARON_NOTIFY against an interactive PATH is how
# the seam looked healthy while being unresolvable where it actually runs:
# ~/bin is on a login PATH and on no --user unit's.
check_notify_seam() {
  _unitpath=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
  _baked=$(sed -n 's/^Environment=CHARON_NOTIFY=//p' \
             "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sync@.service" 2>/dev/null | head -1)
  [ -n "$_baked" ] || return 0
  case "$_baked" in
    /*) [ -x "$_baked" ] && _nok=1 || _nok= ;;
    *)  if (PATH=$_unitpath; command -v "$_baked" >/dev/null 2>&1)
        then _nok=1; else _nok=; fi ;;
  esac
  if [ -z "$_nok" ]; then
    fail_drift "notify seam '$_baked' does NOT resolve on a unit PATH;"\
         "faults raise no flag (re-run install to bake an absolute path)"
    return 1
  fi
  echo "  [OK]   notify seam reachable from a unit: $_baked"
  # List CHARON's flags, not every flag on the box. The seam is shared with
  # whatever else an integrator wires into it, and printing another
  # subsystem's pending flag inside charon's own check reads as though charon
  # raised it.
  echo; echo "== notify ($_baked): charon flags =="
  _nl=$("$_baked" list 2>/dev/null | grep -i charon || true)
  if [ -n "$_nl" ]; then printf '%s\n' "$_nl"
  else echo "  (none)"; fi
  return 0
}

# Remove what install created, and NOTHING else. Explicitly NOT the cache and
# NOT the config: charon manages a cache it did not author, so tearing down the
# machinery must never touch the data or the declarations that describe it.
do_uninstall() {
  log_info "uninstall: removing generated units, timers and profiles"
  for p in $(profile_names); do
    _t=charon-sync-$p.timer
    systemctl --user disable --now "$_t" >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
    rm -f "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/$_t"
    rm -f "$UNISON_DIR/charon-$p.prf"
    _dp=$(sync_dropin_file "$p"); rm -rf "${_dp%/*}"
  done
  rm -f "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sync@.service"
  systemctl --user disable --now charon-sweep.timer >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
  rm -f "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sweep.timer"
  rm -f "$SYSTEMD_USER_DIR/charon-sweep.service"
  systemctl --user daemon-reload >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
  log_info "uninstall: the cache ($CHARON_CACHE) and config are UNTOUCHED"
}

do_check() {
  echo "== charon-sync systemd check =="
  _rc=0
  # Config coherence FIRST, and before the systemd gate: an overlapping source
  # or a duplicated target is wrong whether or not there is a scheduler, and it
  # is the kind of wrong that destroys data rather than merely not running.
  check_config_sanity || _rc=1
  check_source_roots || _rc=1
  if ! have_systemd; then
    fail_fault "systemctl not found: charon schedules through systemd"\
         "--user, so nothing here is running automatically."
    echo "         The reconciler config is still valid; drive"\
         "'${APP_NAME%% *} sync' from your own scheduler."
    return "$EX_FAULT"
  fi

  check_service_template || _rc=1
  _any=
  for p in $(profile_names); do
    _any=1
    check_profile_health "$p" || _rc=1
  done
  [ -n "$_any" ] || echo "  [WARN] no profiles configured ($PROFILES_DIR)"
  # Litter itself is NOT drift (see check_crumbs), but a CRUMB_AGE_MIN charon
  # refuses to act on is: nothing would ever be swept, and silently.
  check_crumbs || _rc=1
  check_twins  || _rc=1
  check_audit  || _rc=$(worse_of "$_rc" "$?")
  check_push_authorisation || _rc=$(worse_of "$_rc" "$?")
  check_notify_seam || _rc=1
  # The VERDICT, not just "did anything fail". _rc is passed only so a finding
  # that failed without classifying itself is caught loudly.
  check_verdict "$_rc"
}

# SOURCEABLE FOR TESTING. Every test until now drove the whole command against
# stubs, which is slow and cannot reach an edge case cheaply. With this, a unit
# test sources the file and calls a function directly with controlled input.
# Executing the script is unaffected: the variable is only ever set by a test.
# shellcheck disable=SC2317  # the dispatch below IS reached when executed
# UNSET IT BEFORE RETURNING. The flag has to be visible to the shell that is
# sourcing this file, but if it survives into the ENVIRONMENT every sibling impl
# a function shells out to (charon-source health / status / seed / probe) hits
# this same guard and exits 0 having done NOTHING, a silent no-op that reads
# as success. Measured: seed_profile "succeeded" without running rclone at all,
# and a gate "passed" because charon-source status no-op'd. Unsetting here makes
# the seam safe no matter how a test sets the variable.
if [ -n "${CHARON_LIB_ONLY:-}" ]; then
  unset CHARON_LIB_ONLY
  return 0 2>/dev/null || exit 0
fi

#### main ####
MODE=$1
[ -z "$MODE" ] && MODE="run"
case "$MODE" in
  run)
    PROFILE=$2
    if [ -z "$PROFILE" ] || [ "$PROFILE" = all ]; then
      run_with_lock all
    elif [ -f "$PROFILES_DIR/$PROFILE.conf" ]; then
      run_with_lock "$PROFILE"
    else
      log_error "unknown profile '$PROFILE'"; usage
    fi
    ;;
  seed)      seed_priority ;;
  seed-full) seed_full ;;
  sweep)     shift; do_sweep "$@" ;;
  audit)     shift; do_audit "$@" ;;
  plan)      shift; do_plan "$@" ;;
  # A GRANT WITH NO REVOKE IS HALF A DECISION. Until 2026-10-05 this verb could
  # only authorise, so undoing it meant deleting a state file that nothing
  # documented. Revoking is always SAFE (it only ever restricts what a pass may
  # do), so unlike the grant it needs no warning about consequences.
  allow-push)
    shift
    _apr=
    case "${1:-}" in --revoke|-r) _apr=y; shift ;; esac
    [ -n "${1:-}" ] || { log_error "allow-push needs a profile name"; exit 2; }
    [ -f "$PROFILES_DIR/$1.conf" ] \
      || { log_error "unknown profile '$1'"; exit 2; }
    if [ -n "$_apr" ]; then
      if push_authorised "$1"; then
        revoke_push "$1" || { log_error "could not revoke it"; exit 1; }
        log_warn "profile '$1' is PULL-ONLY again; the next pass will fill the"\
                 "cache and refuse to write the remote."
      else
        log_warn "profile '$1' was already pull-only; nothing to revoke."
      fi
      exit 0
    fi
    authorise_push "$1" "allow-push, by hand" \
      || { log_error "could not record it"; exit 1; }
    log_warn "profile '$1' may now CREATE AND UPDATE on the remote."
    log_warn "If that cache held a stale restore, the next pass pushes it."
    log_warn "Undo with '${APP_NAME%% *} allow-push --revoke $1'." ;;
  install)   do_install ;;
  uninstall) do_uninstall ;;
  config|status) do_status ;;
  check)     do_check ;;
  *)         usage ;;
esac
exit $?

#### Embedded templates (read by extract_section; never executed) ####

__SECTION_BEGIN__ prf:charon
# Unison preferences for @LABEL@
batch = true
label = @LABEL@
root = @MOUNT_ROOT@
root = @CACHE_ROOT@
silent = true
copyonconflict = true
# Unison's own transfer temps are NEVER content. A run killed mid-transfer
# strands one in the destination dir; with no ignore, the NEXT pass sees it as
# an ordinary new file and REPLICATES it to the other side, so a crumb on the
# cache becomes a crumb on the remote, permanently. Found 2026-09-11: 544 such
# files, an IDENTICAL set on both sides (the proof they were synced, not
# independently stranded), 54 MB each way. Ignoring them makes the sync blind to
# its own litter; sweeping it is an out-of-band job.
ignore = Name @CRUMB_GLOB@
ignore = Name @PROBE_DIR@
# A CONFLICT COPY IS NOT CONTENT EITHER, and this file's notes claimed it was
# already local-only because `prefer` makes the cache the overwritten side.
# False: a twin is local-only for ONE pass, then it is ordinary new local
# content and propagates. 566 of them, 34 MB, measured on the live remote
# 2026-10-04. Two forms, because every conflict after the first in a day is
# named `(conflict #N_on_...)`; a glob for only the first misses all repeats.
# Keeping them local is the point: the twin is a backup of what this box was
# about to lose, which is worth having HERE and worth nothing on the remote,
# where with three nodes the date-based name collides by construction.
ignore = Name @TWIN_GLOB@
ignore = Name @TWIN_GLOB_N@
@PREFER_NOTE@
prefer = @PREFER@
confirmbigdel = true
fastcheck = true
__SECTION_END__ prf:charon


__SECTION_BEGIN__ unit:charon-sync@.service
[Unit]
Description=charon unison sync for profile %i
# Ordering against the mount is NOT here: this template is instanced by
# PROFILE, and which SOURCE a profile draws on is not derivable from %i. It is
# written per instance into charon-sync@%i.service.d/10-source.conf instead.

[Service]
Type=oneshot
Environment=CHARON_REMOTE=@CHARON_REMOTE@
Environment=CHARON_NOTIFY=@CHARON_NOTIFY@
# 75 is charon's SKIPPED (EX_TEMPFAIL): the remote was offline, rate-limited,
# or another pass held the lock, so this pass did no work and the next timer
# retries. Not a failure, so an offline laptop does not accumulate failed
# units; not a success either, which is why check reads ExecMainStatus.
SuccessExitStatus=75
ExecStart=%h/.local/bin/charon sync %i
__SECTION_END__ unit:charon-sync@.service


__SECTION_BEGIN__ unit:charon-sweep.service
[Unit]
Description=charon sweep of orphaned Unison transfer temps

[Service]
Type=oneshot
Environment=CHARON_REMOTE=@CHARON_REMOTE@
# 75 is charon's SKIPPED: a sync pass held the lock, so this sweep did nothing
# and tomorrow's will. Marking it a success keeps a box that happens to be
# mid-pass at the sweep hour from accumulating a failed unit forever, which is
# the same reasoning as the sync unit's own SuccessExitStatus.
SuccessExitStatus=75
ExecStart=%h/.local/bin/charon sweep
# AUDIT THE REMOTE on the same daily tick, because the thing it looks for is
# what manufactured 283 conflicts across three nodes on 2026-10-04 and nothing
# was watching for it. It rides this timer rather than getting its own: one
# listing costs seconds, daily is far more often than a duplicate appears, and a
# second timer is a second thing to be enabled, diffed and explained.
#
# `-` PREFIXED ON PURPOSE. The audit exits non-zero when it FINDS something, and
# an unprefixed failure would latch this unit failed until a human fixed the
# remote, which is the permanently-red-unit noise this project removes wherever
# it appears. The finding is not lost: it is written to the audit record, and
# `charon check` turns it into a FAULT, which is the verdict an integrator
# already consumes.
ExecStart=-%h/.local/bin/charon audit
__SECTION_END__ unit:charon-sweep.service


__SECTION_BEGIN__ timer:charon-sweep.timer
[Unit]
Description=@CADENCE@ charon sweep of orphaned Unison transfer temps

[Timer]
OnCalendar=@CADENCE@
# Persistent=true here, UNLIKE the sync timers. A missed sync is caught by the
# next interval a few minutes later, so replaying it at boot only duplicates
# work; a missed sweep would otherwise wait a whole further period, and a laptop
# that is off at the sweep hour is the normal case rather than the exception.
# It matters more the longer the period, which is why weekly is offered.
Persistent=true
# An hour of jitter because every box sharing a remote runs this, and there is
# no reason for them all to delete the same objects in the same minute. The
# operation is idempotent, so this is tidiness rather than safety.
RandomizedDelaySec=1h
Unit=charon-sweep.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
__SECTION_END__ timer:charon-sweep.timer


__SECTION_BEGIN__ timer:charon-sync.timer
[Unit]
Description=charon sync for the @NAME@ subtree

[Timer]
OnBootSec=@BOOT@
OnUnitActiveSec=@INTERVAL@
Unit=charon-sync@@NAME@.service
Persistent=false
RandomizedDelaySec=@JITTER@

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
__SECTION_END__ timer:charon-sync.timer
